    feeds: ["spending self-awareness", "curbing impulse purchases", "felt cost of money"],
    description: "Cash makes spending viscerally salient, whereas cards decouple purchase from payment and blunt the felt cost. Prelec & Simester's classic auction study found willingness-to-pay could be up to ~100% higher when participants were told to pay by credit card rather than cash. Using cash or debit for discretionary categories reintroduces friction that curbs overspending.",
