    description: "The popular idea (\"talent stack\") that combining several merely-good skills beats mastering one. It has a thin academic base. The closest real evidence is Lazear's (2005) jack-of-all-trades theory: people with balanced, broad skill portfolios are more likely to become entrepreneurs (tested on Stanford alumni). Follow-ups such as Silva (2007) suggest that link may reflect pre-existing tastes or ability rather than acquired breadth causing success.",
