    description: "Janis argued that cohesive, insulated groups under directive leadership suppress dissent, self-censor, and share an illusion of unanimity, producing fiascos (Bay of Pigs, Challenger). Esser's review of ~25 years of research found that historical case analyses fit the model well and that some antecedents — directive leadership, group insulation, and lack of decision procedures — reliably predict defective decision processes. Crucially, laboratory tests provide weak and inconsistent support for the full model, especially the causal role of cohesion.",
