    description: "When individual contributions are pooled and not identifiable, people reduce effort — the whole produces less than the sum of members working alone. Karau & Williams's meta-analysis of 78 studies found a robust, moderate effect (d ≈ 0.44) that generalizes across tasks and populations. Loafing is largest when individual output is not evaluable, the task feels unimportant or redundant, and coworkers are expected to perform well.",
