    degrades: ["Behavior-change success", "Course-correction from feedback", "Goal attainment and follow-through"],
    description: "Control-theory models of self-regulation put progress monitoring between setting a goal and reaching it: without tracking, the feedback loop that catches drift never closes. Harkin and colleagues' meta-analysis of 138 studies (N≈19,951) found that prompting people to monitor goal progress reliably increased goal attainment, with larger effects when progress was recorded and made public. The clear implication is that a weak self-monitoring habit — not tracking diet, spending, workouts or deadlines — is a genuine, modifiable self-regulation weakness that lets goals quietly fail.",
