    description: "Adaptive self-regulation includes knowing when to quit: disengaging effort and commitment from goals that have become unattainable and reengaging with new ones. Wrosch and colleagues show that people low in goal-adjustment capacity — who cannot let go and cannot redirect — report lower subjective wellbeing, and follow-up work links poor disengagement to more distress and worse biological/physical-health regulation (e.g., cortisol, inflammation). This is the opposite failure from giving up too easily: persistence on a dead goal becomes its own self-regulation weakness.",
