    id: "financial-avoidance-ostrich-effect", section: "671", title: "Financial Avoidance / Ostrich Effect → Surprise Insolvency", subtitle: "Degrades: early problem detection, corrective action, solvency",
    evidenceTag: "Emerging",
    weakness: { threat: 5, weakLines: ["Financial", "Volitional"], degree: "moderate contributor", onset: "months", reversibility: "recovers" },
    degrades: ["early detection of problems", "timely corrective action", "solvency"],
    description: "People selectively stop looking at their finances exactly when the news is bad, so small, fixable problems (an overdue bill, a slipping balance) are only discovered once they have compounded into a crisis. Karlsson, Loewenstein & Seppi (2009) documented the 'ostrich effect': investors log in to check their portfolios markedly less often in falling markets than rising ones, avoiding painful information. The same avoidance applied to bank balances, statements, and bills is a plausible pathway to missed deadlines, penalties, and surprise insolvency.",
