    description: "Some people reliably report the first intuitive answer that comes to mind rather than pausing to check it — a disposition Frederick captured with the three-item Cognitive Reflection Test. Low CRT scorers fall for problems engineered to have a compelling-but-wrong intuitive answer (the bat-and-ball problem) and are more prone to reasoning and judgment errors. In learning, this shows up as persistence of intuitive misconceptions that survive instruction because the learner never engages the analytic override.",
