    degrades: ["Choosing genuinely effective study methods", "Evidence-based instructional decisions", "Time and effort allocation"],
    description: "The conviction that one learns best in a fixed modality ('I'm a visual learner') and should be taught to match it — the meshing hypothesis — has no supporting evidence. Pashler et al.'s commissioned review found the crossover interaction the hypothesis predicts is essentially never demonstrated, and studies designed to test it flatly contradict it. Yet Nancekivell et al. found ~89% of the public still endorses learning styles, so learners and teachers pour effort into matching modality instead of matching method to content.",
    callout: "The harm here is opportunity cost and misallocated effort, not direct damage — believing you have a style is mostly benign until it crowds out effective, content-appropriate strategies (spacing, retrieval, dual coding). Rate the threat modest and honest, not alarmist. The myth is durable, but its measured effect on outcomes is small.",
