    id: "bilingualism-cognitive-reserve", section: "815", title: "Bilingualism & Cognitive Reserve — The Contested Claim", subtitle: "Bolsters clusters: Verbal Repertoire; possibly Cognitive Reserve (contested)",
    evidenceTag: "Mixed",
    impact: { magnitude: 2, latency: "weeks", durability: "lasting", effort: "high" },
    feeds: ["A second-language verbal repertoire", "Possibly cognitive reserve / delayed dementia onset (association, contested)"],
    description: "The popular 'bilingual advantage' in executive function largely does not survive scrutiny: a large meta-analysis (Lehtonen et al., 2018; 152 studies, 891 effect sizes) found small advantages for inhibition, shifting, and working memory that vanished after correcting for publication bias — with a small bilingual disadvantage for verbal fluency. Separately, observational work suggests lifelong bilingualism may delay dementia onset ~4-5 years, but this too is confounded and inconsistently replicated.",
