    feeds: ["Tax diversification", "Retirement withdrawal flexibility", "Hedge against future tax increases"],
    description: "Holding both pre-tax (traditional) and after-tax (Roth) accounts hedges uncertainty about future tax rates and lets retirees control taxable income year to year. Brown, Cederburg & O'Doherty (2017, Journal of Financial Economics) modeled a progressive tax schedule plus future-rate uncertainty and found most households do best diversifying between the two, and that Roth's benefit is not limited to low earners (the paper won the 2018 TIAA Samuelson Award). Converting traditional balances to Roth in low-income years (early retirement, market dips) can lock in today's relatively low rates.",
