    impact: { magnitude: 3, latency: "weeks", durability: "sustained", effort: "moderate" },
    feeds: ["exercise motivation and persistence", "accountability and habit maintenance", "companionship during effort", "group belonging"],
    description: "Exercising with others — rather than alone — raises effort and persistence via the Köhler motivation-gain effect: a weaker partner in a conjunctive task works harder to not let the group down. A meta-analysis of ~19 studies (N≈1,912) found a significant motivation gain for partnered vs. individual exercise, moderated by partner type and task. Group formats also add companionship and accountability that support long-run adherence and reduced loneliness.",
    callout: "The strongest, best-replicated effect is on motivation/effort in lab and app settings, not on long-term wellbeing; some trials show group social features do NOT beat solo exercise on loneliness. Benefits require the right task structure (interdependent, not merely co-present).",
