    feeds: ["idea generation volume", "reducing conformity and blocking", "structured creative collaboration"],
    description: "The intuition that group brainstorming out-produces individuals is wrong: nominal groups (people ideating alone, then pooling) reliably generate more and better ideas than interacting brainstorming groups. Diehl & Stroebe (1987) reviewed 22 comparisons and found interacting groups less productive in 18; the main culprit is 'production blocking' (only one person talks at a time), plus evaluation apprehension and free-riding. The fix is to have people write ideas independently first (brainwriting), then converge.",
    callout: "This is one of social psychology's best-replicated findings, but it is about idea quantity/quality in the generation phase; groups still add value for building on, evaluating, and selecting ideas — the lesson is 'diverge alone, converge together,' not 'never collaborate.'",
