    description: "The false consensus effect is the tendency to overestimate how widely others share one's own beliefs, preferences, and choices, projecting the self onto the group (Ross, Greene, & House, 1977). Mullen et al.'s (1985) meta-analysis of 115 hypothesis tests found a reliable effect (average r ≈ 0.30), though modern publication-bias corrections push it toward r ≈ 0.18-0.22. It degrades judgment by making one assume 'everyone thinks like me,' which sabotages negotiation, market/product bets, and reading a room.",
