    id: "anger-emotional-leakage-negotiation", section: "961", title: "Uncontrolled Anger / Emotional Leakage → Relational Damage & Retaliation", subtitle: "Degrades: trust, subjective value, long-term deal relationships",
    evidenceTag: "Mixed",
    weakness: { threat: 5, weakLines: ["Social-Perceptual", "Rhetorical"], degree: "moderate contributor", onset: "immediate", reversibility: "partial" },
    degrades: ["preserving trust and rapport", "protecting long-term deal relationships", "avoiding retaliatory spirals", "subjective-value creation"],
    description: "Poorly regulated emotion in bargaining is double-edged. Moderate expressed anger can extract short-term concessions (counterparts infer a high resistance point), but uncontrolled or high-intensity anger backfires: it is judged inappropriate, triggers retaliation, and reliably damages trust and the subjective/relationship value of the deal — the outcomes that determine whether the counterpart deals with you again. Meta-analytic work confirms emotional expressions shift concessions but negative displays erode relational outcomes.",
