    description: "High-frequency day trading and retail options speculation are, on average, reliably wealth-destroying activities distinct from simple stock-picking. In a 15-year census of the Taiwan market, over 80% of day traders lost money in a typical six-month window and fewer than ~1% predictably earned profits net of costs; aggregate individual-investor losses ran ~2% of GDP. Retail options traders fare no better: multiple studies find them buying overpriced options, paying huge spreads, and losing on the order of 5-16% over short holding periods, with losses roughly tripling around earnings announcements.",
