    description: "Managers who expand headcount, budget, and acquisitions to grow their own power and prestige — rather than to create value — commit the classic free-cash-flow agency problem. Empirical accounting/finance studies show empire-building managers overinvest, disclose less, and destroy shareholder value; firm-level evidence ties weak monitoring and abundant free cash flow to value-reducing growth and acquisitions. At the team level the same drive shows up as turf wars and resource hoarding between units.",
    callout: "This is an agency/incentive problem, not simple incompetence — it is strongest where governance and monitoring are weak and free cash flow is high; effects are documented mainly in large public firms, and 'growth' and 'empire-building' can be hard to separate cleanly.",
