    description: "NIH syndrome is the tendency of a stable, insular group to believe it holds a monopoly on knowledge and to devalue externally sourced ideas irrespective of their objective quality. Katz & Allen's study of 50 R&D project groups linked long, stable group tenure to falling external communication and declining performance after roughly five years. It starves innovation of outside knowledge and blocks open-innovation absorption — a strength (deep internal expertise) curdling into a filter that rejects better outside solutions.",
