    description: "Creative output follows a serial-order effect: early ideas are the obvious, high-accessibility ones, and originality rises the longer generation continues. Because persisting feels unproductive (the 'creative cliff illusion' — people wrongly believe their idea quality is falling when it is actually climbing), people quit at the point where the most original ideas were about to emerge. The weakness is premature termination, not lack of ability: the best material is left ungenerated.",
    callout: "The serial-order/persistence effect is robust in lab divergent-thinking tasks but average-based; individuals vary, and diminishing returns are real at some point, so 'push longer' is a tendency-level heuristic, not an unlimited rule.",
