    description: "Severe perineal trauma (obstetric anal sphincter injury, OASIS) occurs in roughly 3% of vaginal births overall and ~6% of first births. Consequences include anal incontinence (about 10% within a year of OASIS), chronic pelvic pain, and dyspareunia, alongside psychological harm: about one third of women with severe perineal trauma report persisting anxiety and depression at one year, and OASIS is linked to post-traumatic stress symptoms.",
    callout: "Not all traumatic births involve physical injury and not all injuries cause trauma — birth-related PTSD tracks the subjective experience (loss of control, fear, poor care) as much as the tear itself, so physical repair alone does not resolve the psychological cost.",
