    description: "Pelvic floor dysfunction is a common, under-discussed cost of childbearing. Within 10 years of first birth about 50% of women report at least one pelvic floor disorder — ~44% urinary incontinence, ~16% anal incontinence, ~5–6% symptomatic prolapse. Risk of prolapse and stress-incontinence surgery rises consistently with parity after vaginal (but not cesarean) birth, and these disorders erode psychological, physical, social, and sexual wellbeing.",
