    degrades: ["Bodily health (melanoma and non-melanoma skin cancer, photoaging)", "Aesthetic/appearance over the long term", "Financial resources (treatment costs)", "Intrapersonal/mood regulation"],
    description: "A proposed behavioral addiction to ultraviolet exposure, defined by adapting substance-dependence criteria (tolerance, withdrawal, loss of control despite skin-cancer risk). It is NOT a formal DSM diagnosis. Screening surveys estimate dependence in roughly 20-45% of college-age indoor tanners; experimental work shows frequent tanners can discriminate real from mock UV and that naltrexone can induce withdrawal-like symptoms, implicating opioid mechanisms. The core harm is a direct, dose-dependent increase in melanoma and other skin cancers.",
    callout: "Not a recognized diagnosis; the addiction framing rests on adapted criteria and small experimental samples, prevalence swings with the screening tool, and it overlaps with body-image and mood problems - but the downstream skin-cancer risk is well established and largely irreversible.",
