    description: "Micro- and nanoplastics are now detectable in human blood, lung, placenta, and other tissues, and mechanistic work shows they can trigger inflammation and oxidative stress. A 2024 rapid systematic review (UCSF/NASEM-style GRADE) judged microplastics 'suspected' to harm sperm quality and digestive immunity (rated high-quality bodies of evidence) and 'suspected' for several reproductive/respiratory outcomes at moderate quality — but 'unclassifiable' for birth outcomes on low-quality evidence.",
    callout: "Flag heavily — this is the most speculative cluster here. Despite intense media attention, there is NO established causal link between microplastic exposure and any specific human disease: exposure measurement is unstandardized, most data are in-vitro or animal at doses above real-world levels, and human effect sizes are essentially unquantified. Include as a monitored uncertainty, not a demonstrated harm.",
