    impact: { magnitude: 2, latency: "weeks", durability: "sustained", effort: "low" },
    feeds: ["Bodily", "Emotional"],
    description: "The choice of magnesium salt matters more than the label 'magnesium' implies. Magnesium oxide is cheap but poorly absorbed (roughly 4% fractional absorption) and mostly acts as an osmotic laxative; organic forms (citrate, glycinate/bisglycinate) are absorbed several-fold better. A randomized crossover study found citrate raised serum and urinary magnesium more than oxide; glycinate is typically chosen when GI tolerability is the constraint. This addresses form-selection, not whether magnesium repletion helps sleep/anxiety (covered separately).",
