    description: "Calcium supplements produce only small, largely non-progressive bone-density gains (~1-2%) and a borderline ~10-11% reduction in total fracture, with no significant effect on hip fracture in many analyses. Meanwhile Bolland's influential meta-analyses reported that calcium (without or with vitamin D) modestly raised myocardial-infarction risk (~24-27%), a finding that remains contested. Consensus has shifted toward getting calcium from food and reserving supplements for those who cannot meet needs dietarily.",
