    degrades: ["follow-through", "behavior-change execution", "reliability", "habit override"],
    description: "Forming a strong intention is a weak guarantee of doing the thing. Webb & Sheeran's meta-analysis of experimental studies found that a medium-to-large change in intention (d = 0.66) produced only a small-to-medium change in behavior (d+ = 0.36) — roughly half of intenders fail to act. The gap is widest for goals requiring sustained effort, competing with habits, or dependent on a specific opportunity; 'inclined abstainers' (intend but don't act) are the dominant failure mode, not people who never intended.",
    callout: "This is a near-universal design flaw of human volition, not a personal defect; implementation intentions and cue-based planning shrink but do not close it. Effect sizes come from experimental intention-manipulation studies, which may overstate the gap in strongly self-relevant goals.",
