    description: "People fixate on numerators and neglect denominators, so a 9-in-100 chance can feel bigger than 1-in-10 because '9 winners' beats '1 winner.' Denes-Raj & Epstein found many people knowingly bet on the worse odds to have more winning beans in the bowl. The bias tracks low numeracy and distorts how patients read '1 in 1,000' side-effect risks and how gamblers and shoppers read odds and discounts.",
