    id: "vague-goals-low-specificity", section: "1403", title: "Vague 'Do-Your-Best' Goals → Undefined Success, Slack Effort", subtitle: "Degrades: performance intensity, persistence, self-monitoring",
    evidenceTag: "Strong",
    weakness: { threat: 5, weakLines: ["Volitional", "Strategic", "Meta-Cognitive"], degree: "moderate contributor", onset: "immediate", reversibility: "recovers" },
    degrades: ["effort intensity", "persistence", "self-monitoring", "goal-directed focus"],
    description: "Setting a fuzzy 'do my best' goal is one of the most reliable ways to underperform. Across ~1,000 studies, specific and difficult goals beat vague or easy goals, with meta-analytic advantages of roughly d = 0.42–0.80 for specific-hard over do-your-best. Vague goals have no external referent, so people define success idiosyncratically and grant themselves the benefit of the doubt — dampening effort, persistence, and the self-monitoring that specificity enables.",
