    description: "Evaluating options side by side (joint) versus one at a time (separate) flips preferences, so people pick what they'll comparatively over-value but separately under-enjoy. Hsee's less-is-better cases: a $45 scarf seems more generous than a $55 coat; 7 oz of ice cream overflowing a small cup beats 8 oz underfilling a big one — until you see them together. Because we choose in joint mode but live in separate mode, distinction bias produces predictable mischoice.",
