    degrades: ["on-task focus", "self-regulation", "learning depth", "task enjoyment"],
    description: "Choosing to study while a more attractive leisure option beckons doesn't just cost willpower — the foregone temptation degrades the chosen activity itself. Motivational interference is the cognitive, affective, and behavioral impairment of the on-task activity caused by the incentives of the non-chosen alternative: worse mood, more intrusive thoughts, poorer self-regulation, and reduced learning. Widespread in study–leisure conflicts, it explains why 'just working through' a temptation still yields shallow, resentful effort.",
