    description: "Losing a stable job to a plant closing or mass layoff is not a temporary dip: displaced high-tenure workers suffer large, persistent earnings losses of roughly 15-25% that can last 15-20 years, worse when displacement happens in a recession. The scarring is physical too — in Pennsylvania administrative data, mortality in the year after displacement rose 50-100%, and even 20 years out annual death hazards were 10-15% higher, implying about 1.0-1.5 years of lost life expectancy for a worker displaced at 40. This is distinct from generic unemployment scarring and from skill-obsolescence: the trigger is an involuntary firm-side separation, not stale skills.",
