    degrades: ["Financial", "Entrepreneurial", "Street-Smarts", "Existential"],
    description: "'Upcredentialing' — employers demanding a bachelor's degree for jobs long done without one — devalues experience and shuts capable non-graduates out of middle-skill work. Analysis of ~26 million job postings found more than 6 million middle-skill jobs at risk of degree inflation, with stark gaps such as 65% of production-supervisor postings requiring a degree versus only 16% of current supervisors holding one. The cost lands twice: workers with the aptitude are frozen out, and the credential itself loses signaling value as it becomes a screening default. Distinct from occupational licensing (a legal gate) — this is an employer preference.",
