    id: "occupational-licensing-barriers", section: "1460", title: "Occupational Licensing Barriers → Reduced Mobility & Higher Consumer Costs", subtitle: "Degrades: Financial, Entrepreneurial, Street-Smarts",
    evidenceTag: "Mixed",
    harm: { severity: 2, onset: "years", reversibility: "partial" },
    degrades: ["Financial", "Entrepreneurial", "Street-Smarts", "Strategic"],
    description: "About 25-29% of US workers now need a government license to work, up from ~5% in the 1950s. Licensing raises pay ~15-18% for those who hold the credential, but it does so partly by walling off entry — restricting who can practice, raising consumer prices, and reducing job switching and interstate mobility, which weighs on outsiders, career-changers and the formerly incarcerated. The cost here is the barrier itself: a would-be entrant with the skill but not the credential is frozen out. Distinct from credential inflation (employer degree demands) because the gate is legal, not preferential.",
    callout: "Not all costs — licensing genuinely protects quality/safety in some fields; the wage premium partly reflects selection into licensed occupations, so the 'pure barrier' cost is contested.",
