    degrades: ["Financial", "Strategic", "Logical"],
    description: "Narrow bracketing means evaluating each decision in isolation rather than as part of the whole portfolio of choices you face, so locally reasonable picks combine into a globally worse — sometimes stochastically dominated — bundle. Read, Loewenstein and Rabin catalogued how narrow brackets distort risk-taking, consumption, and self-control; Rabin and Weizsäcker proved that a narrow bracketer without constant absolute risk aversion will sometimes choose a combination that is first-order stochastically dominated, and found ~89% of people bracket narrowly with 28% making outright dominated combined choices.",
    callout: "Distinct from single-decision 'narrow framing': the failure here is not integrating multiple simultaneous or repeated decisions. Broad bracketing is not always better (it can dull motivation on self-control tasks). Educational, not financial advice.",
    sources: [
      { cite: "Read, D., Loewenstein, G., & Rabin, M. (1999). Choice bracketing. Journal of Risk and Uncertainty, 19(1-3), 171-197.", note: "Integrates evidence that people fail to bracket broadly across risk, consumption, and self-control, generally lowering utility. Limitation: synthesis across heterogeneous paradigms. [Strong — foundational review]", link: "https://doi.org/10.1023/A:1007879411489", kind: "doi" },
      { cite: "Rabin, M., & Weizsäcker, G. (2009). Narrow bracketing and dominated choices. American Economic Review, 99(4), 1508-1543.", note: "Proves narrow bracketers make stochastically dominated combined choices; empirically ~28% did so and ~89% bracketed narrowly. Limitation: paired-gamble tasks may overstate real-world frequency. [Strong — theory + experiment]", link: "https://doi.org/10.1257/aer.99.4.1508", kind: "doi" },
    ],
