    degrades: ["Strategic", "Leadership", "Systemic", "Meta-Cognitive"],
    description: "Leaders repeatedly fail to prevent disasters they had ample information to foresee — a 'predictable surprise.' Bazerman and Watkins trace these failures to converging psychological (discounting the future, positive illusions), organizational (siloed information, no clear owner), and political (special-interest capture) vulnerabilities that keep foreseeable, low-probability-high-cost threats off the agenda until they detonate. The underlying cognitive engine is bounded awareness: decision-makers systematically overlook readily available, relevant information outside their immediate focus.",
    callout: "The flagship cases (9/11, Enron, financial-crisis exposures) are analyzed with hindsight, which risks over-labeling messy events as cleanly 'predictable.' Treat it as a diagnostic framework backed by case analysis, not a quantified effect; the bounded-awareness lab work is tighter but narrower.",
