    weakness: { threat: 4, weakLines: ["Leadership", "Strategic", "Interpersonal"], degree: "moderate contributor", onset: "immediate", reversibility: "recovers" },
    degrades: ["Leadership", "Strategic", "Interpersonal", "Volitional"],
    description: "Groups take collective action that every member privately opposes because each wrongly assumes the others are in favor and fears rocking the boat. Unlike groupthink (where members actually converge and suppress dissent), the Abilene Paradox is a failure to *manage agreement*: people misread each other's true preferences and silently acquiesce, producing decisions no one endorses. It is underwritten by pluralistic ignorance — the robust finding that individuals systematically misjudge how much others share their private reservations.",
    callout: "Harvey's original account is an anecdotal management essay, not an empirical study; treat the specific mechanism as illustrative. Its empirical backbone is the separate, well-supported literature on pluralistic ignorance.",
