    description: "Leaders earn 'idiosyncrasy credits' — latitude to deviate from group norms — by first conforming and demonstrating competence and loyalty. Influence is spendable capital: a leader who cashes in that credit too fast (repeated unpopular calls, norm violations, or broken expectations) runs the balance to zero and loses the group's willingness to follow, even on sound decisions. The weakness is misjudging the reserve — acting as if legitimacy were unlimited when it is continually re-earned.",
