    id: "sinking-funds-goal-accounts", section: "1518", title: "Sinking Funds / Goal-Based Accounts", subtitle: "practice: 'Bolsters clusters: Financial, Intrapersonal, Volitional'",
    evidenceTag: "Moderate",
    impact: { magnitude: 3, latency: "weeks", durability: "sustained", effort: "low" },
    feeds: ["Financial", "Intrapersonal", "Volitional", "Systemic"],
    description: "Pre-funding known but irregular expenses (insurance premiums, car repair, holidays, property tax) in dedicated, labeled sub-accounts so a lumpy bill never becomes debt. Distinct from an emergency fund (unknown shocks) and from general expense tracking: it smooths predictable volatility. Soman & Cheema (2011) found earmarking plus partitioning money into two labeled envelopes raised savings 72% among low-income workers; visual goal reminders further increased the savings rate.",
    callout: "Educational, not financial advice. Labeling exploits mental accounting, which is a bias — the same rigidity that helps you save can cause suboptimal choices (e.g., holding a 0.5% sinking fund while carrying 20% credit-card debt). Fungibility still matters.",
