    id: "windfall-management", section: "1521", title: "Windfall Management", subtitle: "practice: 'Bolsters clusters: Financial, Intrapersonal, Volitional'",
    evidenceTag: "Moderate",
    impact: { magnitude: 3, latency: "days", durability: "transient", effort: "low" },
    feeds: ["Financial", "Intrapersonal", "Volitional", "Meta-Cognitive"],
    description: "Deliberately routing unexpected money (tax refunds, bonuses, inheritances, rebates) into savings/debt before it is mentally 'spent,' countering a documented bias: windfalls are spent more readily than expected income. Arkes et al. (1994) showed across questionnaire and real-cash studies that unanticipated money is spent/bet more; Milkman & Beshears (2009) found a redeemed $10 grocery coupon boosted spending by $1.59, concentrated on atypical items — inconsistent with lifecycle theory but consistent with mental accounting.",
