    degrades: ["Bodily", "Aesthetic", "Existential"],
    description: "Distinct from ambient sun exposure or tanning dependence as a behavior, the UV radiation emitted by tanning devices is itself an IARC Group 1 carcinogen (classified 2009). Meta-analysis (Boniol et al., 19 studies) found ever-use of sunbeds raised melanoma risk about 20%, and — critically — first use before age 35 was associated with a summary relative risk near 1.87 (a ~75-87% increase), with risk rising with number of sessions. Sunbeds also increase squamous- and basal-cell carcinoma risk. Because melanoma is often lethal when caught late and disproportionately strikes younger people, this is a high-severity, entirely avoidable exposure.",
    callout: "Educational. Observational case-control data carry recall bias and confounding by sun exposure/skin type, but the dose-response, young-age effect and biologic plausibility support causation; the effect is strongest for early-life, high-frequency use. Many jurisdictions now ban tanning-bed use by minors.",
