    id: "occupational-noise-hearing-loss", section: "1570", title: "Noise-Induced Occupational Hearing Loss — Preventable and Permanent", subtitle: "Degrades: Musical, Interpersonal, Social-Perceptual, Linguistic",
    evidenceTag: "Strong",
    harm: { severity: 3, onset: "years", reversibility: "lasting" },
    degrades: ["Musical", "Interpersonal", "Social-Perceptual", "Linguistic"],
    description: "Distinct from age-related presbycusis: chronic exposure to occupational noise irreversibly damages cochlear hair cells, producing a characteristic high-frequency notch and, over years, speech-frequency loss. A meta-analysis of 78 studies (47,814 workers) found high-frequency NIHL prevalence around 34% in cross-sectional data; noise-exposed workers had roughly 6-fold higher odds of hearing loss than unexposed workers in industrial cohorts, rising with years of exposure. WHO estimates occupational noise accounts for a meaningful share of adult-onset hearing loss worldwide. It is entirely preventable (engineering controls, hearing protection) yet, once established, does not recover — and downstream drives social withdrawal and cognitive decline.",
