    description: "Post-viral olfactory dysfunction — sharply highlighted by COVID-19 — leaves a substantial minority with lasting anosmia or parosmia; across viral causes it persists in roughly 30% of cases, and 3–8% of COVID patients still report dysfunction two years on. The loss is far from trivial: it impairs quality of life, distorts eating and nutrition, removes hazard cues (smoke, gas, spoiled food), and strains relationships. Cohorts report high rates of depression (~38–41%) and anxiety, with subjective distress mediating the link between longer duration and depression.",
