    id: "cost-bells-palsy-sequelae", section: "1581", title: "Bell's Palsy Sequelae — When the Face Doesn't Fully Come Back", subtitle: "Degrades: Facial Function & Symmetry, Eating/Drinking/Speech, Self-Image, Social Confidence, Quality of Life",
    evidenceTag: "Moderate",
    harm: { severity: 2, onset: "months", reversibility: "partial" },
    degrades: ["Facial Function & Symmetry", "Eating/Drinking/Speech", "Self-Image", "Social Confidence", "Quality of Life"],
    description: "Most Bell's palsy resolves, but 15–30% of patients have incomplete recovery, leaving lasting sequelae: residual paresis (~29%), contracture (~17%), and synkinesis — involuntary co-contractions (e.g., the eye closing when smiling) affecting roughly 26% at one year. These produce facial asymmetry, difficulty drinking, eating, and speaking, and meaningful psychosocial distress, particularly for women, older patients, and long-standing paralysis. Delayed onset of recovery (beyond 2–4 months) predicts a higher likelihood of permanent sequelae.",
    callout: "Educational, not medical advice. Sequelae rates depend on initial severity and early corticosteroid treatment; most patients recover well, so these figures apply to the incomplete-recovery subset. Synkinesis is manageable (botulinum toxin, facial retraining) but not fully reversible.",
