    description: "Collective efficacy is a neighborhood's social cohesion plus its shared willingness to intervene for the common good (e.g., to stop kids skipping school or loitering). In Sampson's landmark Chicago study (8,782 residents, 343 neighborhoods), low collective efficacy predicted substantially more violence after controlling for prior violence and disadvantage — high-efficacy neighborhoods had crime ~40% below low-efficacy ones. Weak collective efficacy also tracks worse child and health outcomes.",
