    description: "Jameton's moral distress is the strain that arises when you know the ethically right action but institutional, hierarchical, or resource constraints stop you from taking it — distinct from moral injury (which involves perpetrating or witnessing a transgression). Systematic reviews find moderate-to-high moral distress in a large share of nurses, with residue that accumulates via a 'crescendo effect' and predicts burnout and turnover intention; roughly half of surveyed nurses in some samples considered leaving over it.",
