    degrades: ["Logical", "Mathematical", "Financial", "Strategic"],
    description: "People's preferences violate basic logical coherence. In preference reversals, the same person picks the higher-probability P-bet when choosing but assigns a higher dollar value to the higher-payoff $-bet — Lichtenstein & Slovic found this in 51-83% of respondents. Tversky separately showed choices can be intransitive (A>B, B>C, yet C>A) under structured conditions. Incoherent, procedure-dependent preferences mean a person can be money-pumped and cannot reliably optimize any goal with tradeoffs.",
    callout: "Preference reversals are robust and survive monetary incentives; strict intransitivity is more contested — Regenwetter et al. (2011) argue 'unambiguous evidence is currently lacking' at the individual level once measurement error is modelled.",
    sources: [
      { cite: "Lichtenstein, S., & Slovic, P. (1971). Reversals of preference between bids and choices in gambling decisions. Journal of Experimental Psychology, 89(1), 46-55.", note: "51-83% chose the P-bet but placed a higher price on the $-bet — inconsistent with any transitive utility theory. Replicated widely, incl. with incentives. [Strong for reversals]", link: "https://doi.org/10.1037/h0031207", kind: "doi" },
      { cite: "Tversky, A. (1969). Intransitivity of preferences. Psychological Review, 76(1), 31-48.", note: "Demonstrated predictable intransitive choices under specified conditions; later work (Regenwetter et al., 2011) questions individual-level evidence. [Mixed]", link: "https://doi.org/10.1037/h0026750", kind: "doi" },
    ],
