    description: "Time-out from positive reinforcement — a brief, calm, predictable withdrawal of attention/reinforcement after a defined misbehavior, always paired with abundant 'time-in' warmth — is among the most researched and effective discipline procedures. Leijten et al. found teaching time-out was associated with stronger effects specifically in indicated-prevention and treatment contexts. Dadds & Tully's re-analysis rebutted the popular claim that time-out damages attachment or is 'traumatic,' finding the evidence overwhelmingly supports its efficacy and acceptability when done correctly; longitudinal data show no link to worse child emotional/behavioral functioning.",
