    feeds: ["Linguistic", "Parental", "Logical", "Social-Perceptual"],
    description: "Watching high-quality educational content alongside a child and actively talking about it — asking questions, connecting it to real life ('joint media engagement') — turns passive screen time into a learning context. Kearney & Levine's natural experiment on Sesame Street found that childhood access to it improved school performance, especially for boys and disadvantaged children, at roughly a full year of preschool's benefit for the price of ~$5/child/year. Experimental 'dialogic viewing' work shows adult questioning during viewing boosts what young children learn and transfer.",
