    impact: { magnitude: 2, latency: "months", durability: "transient", effort: "moderate" },
    feeds: ["Volitional", "Meta-Cognitive", "Emotional", "Parental"],
    description: "Structured self-regulation games and curricula (Tools of the Mind, plus simpler impulse-control games like Simon Says or Red Light/Green Light) aim to build inhibitory control, working memory and cognitive flexibility. Diamond et al.'s influential Science report showed EF gains from Tools of the Mind, but larger replications are inconsistent — Blair & Raver's cluster-RCT found selective benefits mostly in high-poverty schools, and Jacob & Parkinson's review warns the EF-to-achievement link is largely correlational, not proven causal. Near-transfer to trained skills is real; far-transfer to grades is shaky.",
