    description: "Eleven meta-analyses confirm a robust 'beauty halo': raters agree on who is attractive (within and across cultures) and treat and judge attractive people more positively — a real, measurable social advantage. Crucially, much of it is cultivable: analyzing Add Health, grooming (clothing, hair, makeup, hygiene) accounted for the entire attractiveness-income premium for women and about half for men, with a well-groomed average-attractiveness woman earning ~US$6,000/yr more than a poorly-groomed peer. Neglecting self-presentation thus forfeits social capital and, at the margin, income and mate prospects.",
