    impact: { magnitude: 3, latency: "months", durability: "sustained", effort: "moderate" },
    feeds: ["Systemic", "Strategic", "Entrepreneurial", "Meta-Cognitive", "Mechanical"],
    description: "Systematically moving through roles/tasks (distinct from a single stretch assignment) to broaden skill and system understanding. Campion, Cheraskin & Stevens (1994) found rotation rate predicted faster skill acquisition, salary growth, and promotion in a pharmaceutical finance department. A 2021 meta-analysis ('More Hype Than Substance?') confirmed positive but modest links to learning, motivation, and career outcomes, while flagging that much of the enthusiasm outruns the causal evidence.",
    callout: "Nearly all evidence is observational and self-selected: high performers get rotated more, so rotation-to-promotion links are inflated by reverse causation. Rotation also carries productivity dips during ramp-up.",
