    description: "A group bound by a shared domain who learn from each other through sustained joint practice (Lave & Wenger). Distinct from networking (weak-tie contacts) and mentoring (dyadic): learning is peripheral-to-central participation in a real practice. Reviews in healthcare and higher education find members report faster tacit-knowledge transfer, professional identity formation, and information sharing.",
