    feeds: ["Bodily", "Emotional", "Interoceptive", "Adversarial"],
    description: "The cross-stressor adaptation hypothesis holds that repeated physical stress from training dampens physiological reactivity to psychosocial stressors (e.g. blunted heart-rate/cortisol responses to a Trier-type task). Acute bouts and some training RCTs do reduce reactivity and speed heart-rate recovery, but the chronic-fitness evidence is genuinely mixed: meta-regressions of laboratory studies find little to no general adaptation from fitness per se. This is a distinct mechanism from the well-established mood/cognition benefits of exercise catalogued elsewhere.",
