    id: "deferred-home-maintenance", section: "2055", title: "Deferred-Maintenance Blindness → Escalating Repair & Value Loss", subtitle: "Degrades: household financial foresight, asset preservation, planning",
    evidenceTag: "Moderate",
    weakness: { threat: 4, weakLines: ["Financial", "Strategic"], degree: "moderate contributor", onset: "years", reversibility: "partial" },
    degrades: ["Financial", "Strategic", "Mechanical"],
    description: "Skipping routine upkeep (gutters, roof, HVAC, caulking, water systems) trades small predictable costs now for large unpredictable ones later. Housing-economics work shows structures depreciate meaningfully without upkeep — Harding, Rosenthal & Sirmans estimate ~2.5%/yr depreciation without maintenance versus ~2% with it, and that neglecting maintenance directly erodes home value. Industry loss data echo the mechanism: a deferred repair commonly compounds several-fold, and water-damage claims (about 1 in 60 insured homes annually) are largely maintenance-preventable. The weakness is a foresight/present-bias failure: the cost of inaction is invisible until a system fails catastrophically.",
