    id: "fine-print-nonreading", section: "2057", title: "Fine-Print & Terms-of-Service Blindness → Signing Away Rights", subtitle: "Degrades: contract literacy, self-protection, financial risk control",
    evidenceTag: "Strong",
    weakness: { threat: 4, weakLines: ["Street-Smarts", "Financial"], degree: "major contributor", onset: "months", reversibility: "partial" },
    degrades: ["Street-Smarts", "Financial", "Adversarial"],
    description: "Almost nobody reads the standard-form contracts, EULAs, warranties, and terms they agree to. Bakos, Marotta-Wurgler & Trossen tracked 48,154 shoppers at 90 software sites and found only 1-2 per 1,000 even opened the license agreement, and those who did read a tiny fraction. The limiting factor is not access cost but the effort of reading and comprehending. This blindness lets sellers embed arbitration clauses, auto-renewals, data-sharing, and fee terms that consumers 'consent' to without knowledge — surfacing later as surprise charges, waived rights, or lost recourse.",
    callout: "The behavior is near-universal and rational at the margin (opportunity cost of reading dense legalese is huge), so 'just read everything' is not realistic advice; the real cost is asymmetric terms, not laziness per se.",
