    description: "Repeated statements are judged more true than novel ones, because processing fluency is misread as a signal of accuracy. A meta-analysis of the truth effect found a robust repetition-based increase in perceived truth, and later work showed it operates even when people possess the correct knowledge and even for implausible claims. This is the cognitive engine behind propaganda, ad repetition, and viral misinformation.",
