    id: "cost-statin-intolerance-consequence", section: "2178", title: "Statin Intolerance & Its Downstream Cost", subtitle: "Degrades: Bodily, Financial, Meta-Cognitive, Volitional",
    evidenceTag: "Mixed",
    harm: { severity: 3, onset: "years", reversibility: "partial" },
    degrades: ["Bodily", "Financial", "Meta-Cognitive", "Volitional"],
    description: "Statin intolerance — usually muscle symptoms — leads many patients to stop a therapy that lowers cardiovascular events, and discontinuation is associated with more heart attacks, strokes and higher mortality. The honest twist: true intolerance is uncommon (meta-analytic prevalence ~7–9%; complete intolerance ~3–5%), and blinded n-of-1 and trial-crossover data show the large majority of symptoms are nocebo effects (negative expectation), not the drug. The 'cost' here is as much a psychological/expectancy failure as a pharmacological one.",
    callout: "Educational, not medical advice. This is a case where the harm is largely from unnecessary discontinuation driven by misattributed symptoms; systematic rechallenge, dose adjustment, or alternative lipid-lowering usually preserves protection. Do not start/stop statins without clinician guidance.",
