    degrades: ["Emotional", "Interpersonal", "Rhetorical"],
    description: "King & Emmons's Ambivalence over Emotional Expressiveness Questionnaire captures a specific internal conflict — wanting to express a feeling yet fearing the consequences, then ruminating after you do ('after I express anger it bothers me for a long time'). This is distinct from habitual suppression (which is a strategy) and from alexithymia (which is not knowing the feeling): here the emotion is known and the urge to express is present, but blocked by conflict. Ambivalence over expression is associated with elevated psychological distress, depression, and anxiety across clinical and non-clinical samples.",
