    weakness: { threat: 6, weakLines: ["Volitional", "Meta-Cognitive", "Strategic"], degree: "major contributor", onset: "immediate", reversibility: "recovers" },
    degrades: ["Volitional", "Meta-Cognitive", "Strategic"],
    description: "Nordgren and colleagues documented the restraint bias: people systematically overestimate their capacity to control visceral impulses (hunger, craving, arousal), and this inflated impulse-control belief leads them to overexpose themselves to temptation — the very exposure that causes them to succumb. In their studies, higher self-rated restraint predicted choosing more tempting environments; critically, recovering smokers with more inflated impulse-control beliefs exposed themselves to more smoking cues and had HIGHER relapse rates four months later. The mechanism is meta-cognitive overconfidence, distinct from raw low self-control: it is confidence, not weakness, that drives the failure.",
