    degrades: ["Interpersonal", "Leadership", "Community"],
    description: "Adult attachment travels into the workplace. Avoidantly attached people use 'deactivating' strategies — minimizing dependence, avoiding closeness, not asking for help — which in teams means low instrumental support-seeking, thinner workplace friendships, weaker organizational citizenship, and readier withdrawal or turnover. Richards & Schat (2011) found attachment avoidance predicted reduced support-seeking, lower OCB, and higher turnover intentions beyond the Big Five, and a 2024 'Attachment at Work' meta-analysis links insecure attachment to worse relational and performance outcomes. This is a distinct applied lens from general romantic insecure attachment: it is specifically the deactivation of the support-and-collaboration system in goal-directed groups.",
    callout: "Workplace attachment is usually measured by importing romantic-attachment scales, and evidence is cross-sectional, so reverse causation (bad team experiences amplify avoidance) is plausible. Avoidance also has upsides — self-reliance and composure under stress — so it is not uniformly a deficit.",
