    description: "Most people rate their personal risk of disease as below that of a comparable peer — the 'it won't happen to me' bias. Weinstein's classic community sample found optimistic bias for the large majority of health hazards, and Shepperd et al.'s stocktake found unrealistic optimism replicated across a thousand-plus studies and dozens of conditions. The bias grows precisely for hazards seen as controllable or preventable, blunting the felt need to screen, vaccinate, or change behavior. This is a distinct risk-perception distortion, not the insurance/preparation framing already catalogued.",
