    description: "When a task is novel or complex and you don't yet have the strategy, set a specific learning goal ('discover and use N effective methods') rather than a performance goal ('hit this number'). Seijts & Latham show that on complex tasks a performance goal diverts scarce attention toward outcomes and can hurt, whereas a learning goal directs attention to strategy discovery and improves results; on simple, well-mastered tasks the reverse holds.",
