    id: "process-simulation", section: "2350", title: "Process Simulation (Mentally Rehearsing the Steps)", subtitle: "practice: 'Bolsters Volitional, Strategic, Emotional, Meta-Cognitive'",
    evidenceTag: "Moderate",
    impact: { magnitude: 2, latency: "days", durability: "transient", effort: "low" },
    feeds: ["Volitional", "Strategic", "Emotional", "Meta-Cognitive"],
    description: "Mentally rehearse the process of reaching a goal (when/where/how you'll act) rather than fantasising about the outcome. Pham & Taylor found students who simulated the steps of studying, 5-7 days before an exam, studied more and scored higher — an effect mediated by better planning and lower anxiety — whereas outcome simulation ('imagine the great grade') did not help. It is the mechanism-honest cousin of vision-boarding.",
