    id: "correlation-neglect-risk", section: "2465", title: "Correlation Neglect → Treating Linked Risks as Independent", subtitle: "Degrades: Financial, Mathematical, Strategic",
    evidenceTag: "Moderate",
    weakness: { threat: 5, weakLines: ["Financial", "Mathematical", "Strategic"], degree: "major contributor", onset: "immediate", reversibility: "recovers" },
    degrades: ["Financial", "Mathematical", "Logical", "Strategic"],
    description: "People systematically neglect that signals and risks are correlated, double-counting redundant information and underestimating how much things move together. Enke & Zimmermann showed experimentally that many subjects update beliefs from correlated sources as if independent, producing over-confident, over-swinging beliefs from the mere 'telling and re-telling of stories.' The same blind spot makes people underweight correlated tail risks — an under-diversified portfolio, several 'independent' insurers that all fail in the same disaster, or supposedly separate bets that crash together.",
