    degrades: ["Logical", "Strategic", "Financial", "Street-Smarts"],
    description: "In Slovic's psychometric paradigm, lay risk perception loads on two factors — 'dread' (uncontrollable, catastrophic, involuntary, fatal) and 'unknown' (unobservable, novel, delayed) — rather than on actual death counts. Hazards high on these factors (nuclear power, terrorism, plane crashes) are wildly overweighted while familiar high-mortality hazards (driving, poor diet) are underweighted, so people misallocate fear and protective effort. Gigerenzer estimated that Americans avoiding air travel after 9/11 and driving instead caused roughly 1,595 additional road deaths in the following year — more than the passengers killed on the four planes.",
