    description: "The Social Amplification of Risk Framework (Kasperson and colleagues) describes how a hazard's real magnitude is transformed as its 'signal' passes through media, institutions, and social groups — amplified for some risks (nuclear waste, food scares) and attenuated for others (routine but deadly hazards). Amplification produces secondary 'ripple effects': stigma, loss of trust, market collapse, and demands for costly regulation out of proportion to physical harm. People embedded in high-amplification channels end up with risk priorities driven by social signal strength rather than by expected harm.",
