    description: "Worry appears to draw on a limited emotional budget: when concern about one threat rises, concern about others falls, regardless of whether the other risks changed. This 'finite pool of worry' explains why anxiety about climate, chronic disease, or long-term financial risk collapses when an acute crisis (a recession, a pandemic, a war) captures attention. A large UK study found that as COVID-19 worry rose in 2020, climate-change concern measurably declined, consistent with displacement rather than independent tracking of each risk's true magnitude.",
