    description: "One of Moore & Healy's three distinct overconfidence types: overplacement is the belief that you rank above most peers. On easy/common tasks (driving, being ethical) most people place themselves above average — a logical impossibility for the majority. Crucially, Moore & Healy showed the direction reverses with difficulty: on hard tasks people overestimate their raw score yet underplace themselves relative to others. So overplacement is not a blanket 'everyone thinks they're great' — it is task-dependent and driven by differential regression and noisy self/other knowledge.",
