    description: "Bohnet and Zeckhauser find people demand a higher probability of a good outcome before trusting a person than before taking a statistically identical gamble against nature — the extra premium is the sting of being betrayed by another's choice. This 'betrayal aversion' makes people decline mutually beneficial trust-based deals they would accept if the same risk were impersonal. The pattern replicated across Brazil, China, Oman, Switzerland, Turkey and the US, though its size differs by culture.",
