    description: "Memories of learning shocking news (9/11, a death) feel photographic and are held with unusual, stable confidence. Talarico & Rubin tracked Duke students' 9/11 memories versus everyday memories and found consistency (actual accuracy) decayed identically for both — flashbulb memories were not more accurate. What differed was confidence, vividness, and belief in accuracy, which stayed high only for the flashbulb memory. The danger is a durable subjective certainty completely decoupled from actual fidelity, so you defend a distorted recollection as if it were a photograph.",
