    description: "Emotional granularity (negative emotion differentiation) is the ability to parse feelings into precise categories rather than an undifferentiated blob of 'bad.' People low in granularity default to blunt, maladaptive regulation — binge drinking, aggression, self-injury — because they can't identify what they feel or why. Seah and Coifman's meta-analysis (17 studies) found low negative-emotion differentiation reliably linked to maladaptive behavior (r=-.15), independent of overall negative affect.",
