    description: "Facing thousands of buyable stocks, individual investors solve the search problem by buying whatever grabs attention — names in the news, with abnormal volume, or with extreme one-day returns — and are net buyers of exactly those stocks. Selling shows no such pattern because they only sell what they already own. Institutions using systematic screens do not chase attention (value managers even net-buy on low-volume days), which isolates the retail bias. Buying a stock because it is loud, not because it is cheap, tends to buy at inflated, post-run-up prices.",
