    description: "Physical activity delivers far less weight loss than the naive 'calories burned' model predicts, for two compensating reasons. Physiologically, the body offsets roughly a quarter of exercise energy expenditure (constrained-energy findings show only ~72% of activity calories add to total daily burn). Behaviorally, people who believe they burned a lot then eat more and rest more; overweight adults have overestimated their exercise expenditure by ~72% while underestimating food intake. Lab studies of post-exercise eating, however, show only a small, often nonsignificant intake increase.",
