    description: "Polivy & Herman's false-hope syndrome describes over-optimistic expectations about the speed, amount, ease, and reward of self-change. Diets and resolutions don't simply fail — they succeed briefly, and that early success plus the surge of control and optimism drives repeated over-ambitious restarts despite a history of collapse. Each cycle further erodes self-efficacy while feeling, at the moment of restart, like proof that 'this time is different.' This is the dark twin of the temporal-landmark 'fresh start' — the same restart impulse turned into a churn.",
