    description: "People systematically judge their own risk of negative health events (heart disease, cancer, addiction) as lower than the average peer's — the comparative optimism or optimistic bias documented by Weinstein (1980, 1987). This 'it won't happen to me' appraisal is one reason people under-invest in prevention, screening, and quitting. The bias itself is very well replicated; its causal link to specific risky behavior is real but modest and inconsistent.",
