    description: "Stripped of voice and face, text tilts receivers toward reading messages as more emotionally negative — or flatly neutral — than the sender intended, a receiver-side counterpart to senders' overconfidence in their own tone. Byron's framework catalogs the channel features (absent nonverbal cues, asynchrony, low immediacy) that make emotional miscommunication likely and shows the drift is systematically toward negativity in ambiguous cases. Small sournesses compound into eroded workplace trust.",
    callout: "Byron (2008) is a conceptual review, not a single effect size; the negativity drift is well-supported qualitatively but its magnitude varies by relationship, cues used (emoji, punctuation), and reader mood.",
    sources: [
      { cite: "Byron, K. (2008). Carrying too heavy a load? The communication and miscommunication of emotion by email. Academy of Management Review, 33(2), 309-327.", note: "Theoretical model: email receivers tend to interpret messages as more negative or neutral than intended; identifies moderating channel and individual factors. Conceptual review, not an empirical effect size. [Moderate — review]", link: "https://doi.org/10.5465/amr.2008.31193163", kind: "doi" },
      { cite: "Kruger, J., Epley, N., Parker, J., & Ng, Z.-W. (2005). Egocentrism over e-mail: Can we communicate as well as we think? Journal of Personality and Social Psychology, 89(6), 925-936.", note: "Senders overestimated how well sarcasm/tone would be decoded from text; receivers were near chance. Complements the negativity drift from the sender side. [Moderate]", link: "https://doi.org/10.1037/0022-3514.89.6.925", kind: "doi" },
    ],
