    description: "In the referential-communication paradigm (Krauss & Glucksberg), and its eye-tracking revival by Keysar and colleagues, both speakers and listeners fail to properly restrict reference to shared common ground. Even when speakers explicitly know an addressee cannot see an object, their eyes and descriptions still leak private information; listeners momentarily interpret instructions using objects the speaker could not have meant. We model the other mind less than we assume, especially under time pressure.",
