    weakness: { threat: 3, weakLines: ["Linguistic", "Meta-Cognitive", "Aesthetic"], degree: "moderate contributor", onset: "immediate", reversibility: "recovers" },
    degrades: ["visual recognition", "eyewitness identification", "intuitive expertise", "perceptual memory"],
    description: "Putting a hard-to-verbalize experience — a face, a wine, an intuition — into words can impair the underlying perceptual memory. Schooler and Engstler-Schooler's participants who described a robber were worse at picking him out of a lineup; a large multi-lab Registered Replication (Alogna et al., ~2,000 participants) confirmed the effect but at a smaller size (about 4% immediate, 16% after a delay). It is real yet fragile, appearing mainly when verbalization is immediate and the task requires fine holistic discrimination.",
