    description: "People systematically predict they will act more generously, kindly, and honestly than their peers — and the gap comes not from cynicism about others but from inflated forecasts of their own conduct. Epley & Dunning (2000) found peers' behavior was predicted accurately while self-predictions overshot, because people refuse to apply base rates to themselves. This self-righteous miscalibration blinds you to your own likely lapses and fuels contempt for others who fail where you assume you wouldn't.",
