    description: "People perceive moral agency (capacity to do right/wrong) and moral patiency (capacity to be helped or harmed) as roughly mutually exclusive: whoever is cast as a 'doer' is seen as less able to suffer, and a designated 'victim' is seen as less capable of moral action. Across seven studies Gray & Wegner (2009) showed this produces perverse judgments — e.g. willingness to inflict more pain on a good-doer than on a passive person, and reduced empathy for those we frame as powerful agents. It quietly corrupts fairness, blame allocation, and who we think 'deserves' care.",
