    description: "When a value is treated as 'sacred' (life, loyalty, honor, group identity), even contemplating trading it off against a secular good like money triggers moral outrage, refusal to compute, and compensatory 'moral cleansing' rather than analysis. Tetlock et al. (2000) showed people react to taboo trade-offs with anger and to 'forbidden base rates' (e.g. actuarially valid but taboo statistics) by punishing whoever uses them. This can protect genuine values — but it also blocks necessary cost-benefit reasoning, ossifies negotiation, and makes rational compromise feel like betrayal.",
