    id: "perseveration-set-shifting", section: "2808", title: "Perseveration — Repeating the Old Rule After the Rules Changed", subtitle: "Degrades: Strategic, Adversarial, Systemic, Creative",
    evidenceTag: "Moderate",
    weakness: { threat: 6, weakLines: ["Strategic", "Adversarial", "Systemic", "Creative"], degree: "major contributor", onset: "immediate", reversibility: "partial" },
    degrades: ["cognitive flexibility", "adaptive strategy switching", "responding to changed conditions"],
    description: "The Wisconsin Card Sorting Test measures set-shifting: sort by a hidden rule (color/form/number), then the rule silently changes and you must switch. Perseverative errors — continuing to apply the now-wrong rule despite negative feedback — index cognitive rigidity and are markedly elevated after prefrontal (especially dorsolateral) damage and in several clinical conditions. The same failure mode, in milder form, is why a strategy that once worked keeps getting reapplied to a changed environment where it now loses.",
    callout: "WCST is a clinical/neuropsychological instrument; strong perseveration reflects frontal dysfunction, so severity ranges from mild individual differences in healthy people to marked deficits in patient groups. It taps executive flexibility broadly, not creativity per se.",
