    description: "Hinds (1999) had experts estimate how long a novice would take to learn cell-phone tasks; experts substantially underestimated — novices actually took roughly twice as long — and standard debiasing prompts failed to fix it. Because experts cannot easily reconstruct the naive state, they mis-scope instructions, skip steps in designs, and build products/explanations calibrated to themselves. This 'expert blind spot' quietly collapses teaching, onboarding, user-centered design, and any innovation whose value depends on reaching non-experts.",
