    impact: { magnitude: 2, latency: "weeks", durability: "sustained", effort: "low" },
    feeds: ["Interpersonal", "Community", "Emotional", "Parental"],
    description: "Deliberate, repeated, symbolically meaningful shared practices (a nightly check-in, a private greeting, a standing date) function as 'rituals of connection.' Garcia-Rada, Sezer, and Norton's studies found couples who share meaningful rituals report more positive emotion, higher satisfaction, and greater commitment than those who do not — but only when BOTH partners construe the behavior as a symbolic ritual rather than a mere routine.",
    callout: "Effects come from consumer-research surveys and lab experiments, not long-term couples RCTs; the benefit is fragile — if one partner sees it as just a habit, the effect disappears. The widely cited Gottman 'rituals of connection' claims are clinical/observational, not experimentally verified.",
    sources: [
      { cite: "Garcia-Rada, X., Sezer, O., & Norton, M. I. (2019). Rituals and nuptials: The emotional and relational consequences of relationship rituals. Journal of the Association for Consumer Research, 4(2), 185-197.", note: "Surveys + experiments: shared rituals raise positive emotion, satisfaction, commitment — only when jointly seen as meaningful ritual. Limitation: mostly correlational/short-term. [Emerging]", link: "https://doi.org/10.1086/702761", kind: "doi" },
    ],
