    id: "executive-derailment-failure-to-adapt", section: "2912", title: "Executive Derailment → Yesterday's Strengths Become Fatal Flaws", subtitle: "Degrades: leadership continuity, relationship capital, strategic adaptability, promotion ROI",
    evidenceTag: "Moderate",
    weakness: { threat: 6, weakLines: ["Leadership", "Interpersonal", "Meta-Cognitive"], degree: "major contributor", onset: "years", reversibility: "partial" },
    degrades: ["leadership continuity", "relationship capital", "strategic adaptability", "promotion ROI", "learning agility"],
    description: "Derailment is the pattern of once-successful, high-potential executives stalling or being pushed out near the top. McCall & Lombardo's foundational CCL research found the most frequent cause was insensitivity to others — under stress the derailed became abrasive and intimidating — alongside arrogance, betraying trust, over-managing/failure to delegate, inability to think strategically or adapt to a new boss, and, critically, a broken pattern of learning from assignments. Nearly every derailed manager had relationship problems.",
    callout: "Foundational work is interview/qualitative on small, historically all-male executive samples, so effect sizes aren't quantified and generalizability is limited; later studies (e.g., Lombardo, Ruderman & McCauley) broadened it but the core evidence remains descriptive.",
