    description: "Handing the top job to a relative of the departing CEO, founder, or large shareholder shrinks the candidate pool to a family of one or two — and it shows in the numbers. Pérez-González's AER study of CEO successions finds firms that appoint family-related CEOs underperform unrelated-CEO firms on operating profitability and market-to-book, with the shortfall concentrated where the heir did NOT attend a selective college — 'wasteful nepotism' that limits labor-market competition for the role.",
