    id: "lmx-differentiation-leader-favoritism", section: "2917", title: "LMX Differentiation → Leader Favoritism That Fractures the Team", subtitle: "Degrades: team cohesion, procedural-justice climate, out-group performance, collective solidarity",
    evidenceTag: "Mixed",
    weakness: { threat: 5, weakLines: ["Leadership", "Interpersonal", "Systemic"], degree: "moderate contributor", onset: "months", reversibility: "recovers" },
    degrades: ["team cohesion", "procedural-justice climate", "out-group performance", "collective solidarity"],
    description: "When a leader forms sharply unequal relationships across a team — a trusted in-group with access and resources, an out-group left with less — the visible differentiation corrodes group harmony. Yu, Matta & Cornfield's meta-analysis (4,114 workgroups, 21,745 individuals) finds LMX differentiation consistently NEGATIVELY related to emergent states like cohesion and collective solidarity, with out-group members receiving less trust, respect, and support.",
    callout: "Genuinely mixed: differentiation can be efficient/fair when it tracks real contribution and the justice climate is strong, so effects on performance are contingent, not uniformly bad. This is the leader-side allocation failure — distinct from an individual's own low-quality upward relationship.",
