    impact: { magnitude: 2, latency: "weeks", durability: "transient", effort: "low" },
    feeds: ["Bodily", "Volitional", "Meta-Cognitive"],
    description: "Letting learners choose aspects of practice (when to receive feedback, task order, equipment) is a central 'motivation' pillar of Wulf & Lewthwaite's OPTIMAL theory, and older meta-analyses reported a moderate learning benefit (d ~0.5-0.6 on delayed retention). However, a 2023 bias-corrected meta-analysis found the effect is substantially inflated by reporting bias and underpowered designs, and largely evaporates once corrected.",
