    id: "target-date-funds", section: "2994", title: "Target-Date Funds as the Default Vehicle", subtitle: "practice: 'Bolsters clusters: Automatic Enrollment & Smart Defaults, Automated & Index Investing, Naive 1/N Diversification'",
    evidenceTag: "Strong",
    impact: { magnitude: 3, latency: "months", durability: "sustained", effort: "low" },
    feeds: ["Financial", "Volitional", "Strategic"],
    description: "A single fund that holds an age-appropriate, automatically de-risking mix of global stocks and bonds and rebalances itself. For the inattentive or unsophisticated default investor — the majority of 401(k) participants — TDFs deliver broad diversification, automatic rebalancing, and a glidepath in one decision, sharply reducing common errors like under-diversification, employer-stock concentration, and performance-chasing. Parker, Schoar, and Sun (2023) document that the rise of TDFs moved a large share of retirement savers into disciplined, macro-contrarian (buy-when-stocks-fall) behavior they would rarely execute on their own.",
    callout: "Educational, not financial advice. Glidepaths and expense ratios vary widely between providers; a one-size-fits-all TDF may be too conservative or too aggressive for a specific household, and holding one in a taxable account can be tax-inefficient.",
    sources: [
      { cite: "Parker, J. A., Schoar, A., & Sun, Y. (2023). Retail Financial Innovation and Stock Market Dynamics: The Case of Target Date Funds. Journal of Finance, 78(5), 2673-2723.", note: "TDFs give inattentive investors age-appropriate exposure and move savers into automatic contrarian rebalancing they wouldn't do alone. Limitation: focuses on market dynamics, not individual outcome optimality. [Strong — top-journal empirical]", link: "https://doi.org/10.1111/jofi.13258", kind: "doi" },
    ],
