    degrades: ["Pattern-Recognition", "Logical", "Meta-Cognitive"],
    description: "Availability bias in clinical judgment is the pull toward whichever diagnosis comes to mind most easily — usually one seen recently or vividly — rather than the most probable one. In a controlled experiment, Mamede and colleagues (2010) primed internal-medicine residents with earlier cases and showed their later diagnoses drifted toward the primed (wrong) diagnosis; deliberate reflective reasoning partially reversed it. This is expert intuition misfiring on recency, not ignorance.",
