    description: "The Ovsiankina effect is the spontaneous urge to resume an interrupted task, even when a better use of the moment is available. Ovsiankina's 1928 work under Lewin found ~67% resumption of interrupted activities; a 2025 meta-analysis of the Zeigarnik and Ovsiankina effects reports the resumption effect replicates more reliably than the memory (recall) effect. As a productivity failure, this tension-driven pull can yank attention back to a half-finished low-priority task instead of the plan's current top item, quietly re-sequencing your day around whatever was last interrupted.",
