    description: "Makary and Daniel (2016) famously extrapolated ~251,000 US deaths/year from medical error, ranking it the 'third leading cause of death.' The figure is widely cited but heavily criticized: it averaged a handful of small studies (~35 in-hospital deaths of extrapolatable detail), gave equal weight to unequal samples, performed no formal meta-analysis, and conflated 'adverse event' with 'preventable death caused by error.' More rigorous work puts preventable in-hospital deaths far lower — a real and serious problem, but not clearly the third-leading cause.",
