    feeds: ["Community", "Emotional", "Parental", "Interpersonal"],
    description: "Frequent family meals are associated with lower rates of disordered eating, substance use, and depression, and with higher self-esteem and school success (Harrison et al. review). But almost all of the evidence is cross-sectional and correlational. Crucially, models that adjust for family connectedness or family-level confounders are far less likely to show significant meal effects — suggesting the meals are largely a marker of an already-connected family rather than an independent cause.",
