    description: "Recording a social/speech task and watching it back — with cognitive preparation (predict what you'll see, then watch yourself as if a stranger) — to correct the distorted, exaggerated negative self-image that maintains social anxiety. It is a component of the well-evidenced Clark & Wells cognitive therapy for social anxiety disorder. Experimental trials show cognitive preparation robustly improves the accuracy of self-perceived performance.",
