    id: "cbt-decatastrophizing-downward-arrow", section: "3241", title: "Decatastrophizing / Downward-Arrow", subtitle: "practice: 'Bolsters clusters: Meta-Cognitive, Emotional'",
    evidenceTag: "Emerging",
    impact: { magnitude: 2, latency: "weeks", durability: "transient", effort: "low" },
    feeds: ["Meta-Cognitive", "Emotional"],
    description: "Following a worry to its feared endpoint ('and then what?') and then re-appraising how likely, how bad, and how survivable that outcome actually is, along with coping options. The downward-arrow and catastrophizing-interview procedures reliably surface core fears in experimental work, and decatastrophizing is a standard ingredient of CBT for GAD and panic. Direct efficacy evidence for the isolated technique is thin — it is almost always embedded in a larger protocol.",
