    description: "Central post-stroke pain (historically Dejerine–Roussy / thalamic pain syndrome) is neuropathic pain from the stroke lesion itself, typically contralateral burning, aching, or cold-allodynic pain with disturbed temperature sensation. A 2020 systematic review and meta-analysis of 69 papers put pooled prevalence at about 11% (95% CI 7–18%) across stroke of any location, rising above 50% in medullary or thalamic strokes. Onset can coincide with the stroke (~26%) or emerge over the first year (~41%), and it is often refractory to standard analgesics.",
