    description: "Nociplastic pain is the third mechanistic pain descriptor adopted by the IASP (2017), defined as pain arising from altered nociception without clear tissue damage or neuropathic lesion — the substrate of central sensitization, with hyperalgesia, allodynia, expanded receptive fields, and impaired descending inhibition. Estimated prevalence is roughly 5–15% of the general population, and central-sensitization features appear in 10–40% of people with osteoarthritis, rheumatoid arthritis, spondyloarthritis, and lupus. It commonly co-travels with fatigue, sleep disruption, and cognitive 'fog'.",
