    description: "Optimizing for brand-name prestige or extrinsic status over person-environment fit predictably erodes the outcomes people actually want. Kristof-Brown, Zimmerman & Johnson's (2005) meta-analysis (172 studies, 836 effect sizes) links person-job and person-organization fit to higher satisfaction and performance and lower turnover and strain. Nye, Su, Rounds & Drasgow (2012) find interest congruence — the fit between you and the work — predicts performance and persistence better than interest level alone.",
    callout: "Prestige is not worthless — it can open doors, signal quality, and pay more, and fit studies are correlational (satisfied people may rate fit higher, reverse causation). The failure is treating prestige as a proxy for fit when they diverge, then absorbing chronic misfit costs.",
