    description: "Distinct from anchoring the number: many candidates accept the first offer without countering. Marks & Harold (2011), surveying 149 newly hired employees, found those who negotiated raised starting pay by about $5,000 on average, and that assertive (competing/collaborating) strategies paid off while accommodating/compromising ones did not. Because raises and future offers often build off the starting base, a skipped negotiation compounds over a career.",
