    description: "Students and professionals stay on an ill-fitting degree or major because of years, tuition, and identity already invested — a textbook sunk-cost error. Arkes & Blumer (1985) demonstrated the sunk-cost effect experimentally (people honor prior spending even when it lowers expected value). Staw (1976) showed personal responsibility for the prior commitment intensifies escalation. Applied to education, the already-spent time and money are irrecoverable and should not drive whether to switch.",
