    description: "Treating passion as a fixed thing to be 'found' rather than developed sets a brittle expectation. O'Keefe, Dweck & Walton (2018, 5 studies) showed a fixed theory of interest narrows people to existing interests and makes them anticipate 'boundless motivation,' so interest collapses when the new pursuit gets hard — whereas a growth theory sustains engagement. Newport argues 'follow your passion' misleads people to chase pre-existing passion instead of building rare, valuable skills that then generate passion.",
