    impact: { magnitude: 3, latency: "days", durability: "transient", effort: "low" },
    feeds: ["Meta-Cognitive", "Strategic", "Systemic"],
    description: "When you switch tasks with the first one unfinished, part of your attention stays behind ('attention residue') and degrades performance on the next task. Writing a brief one-minute note on where you left off and what to do next (a 'ready-to-resume' plan) before switching cuts that residue. Across Leroy & Glomb's lab studies, planners focused better on the interrupting task; in one hiring task they were markedly more likely to pick the top candidate than non-planners.",
