    impact: { magnitude: 3, latency: "days", durability: "transient", effort: "low" },
    feeds: ["Strategic", "Meta-Cognitive", "Systemic"],
    description: "People chronically underestimate how long tasks take because they picture the task as one block. Forcing yourself to enumerate the sub-steps before estimating ('unpacking' or task segmentation) pulls estimates upward toward reality. Kruger & Evans found unpacking cut the planning-fallacy bias by more than half, and segmentation studies replicate the direction of the effect.",
    callout: "Moderate: the effect is on time ESTIMATES, not on how fast work actually gets done; over-unpacking can also inflate estimates past the true time. Effect is per-estimate, not a durable skill unless practiced.",
