    id: "cost-methanol-poisoning", section: "3306", title: "Methanol Poisoning", subtitle: "Degrades: vision, acid-base homeostasis, CNS integrity, survival",
    evidenceTag: "Strong",
    harm: { severity: 5, onset: "immediate", reversibility: "lasting" },
    degrades: ["vision", "acid-base homeostasis", "CNS integrity", "survival", "Spatial"],
    description: "Methanol itself is mildly intoxicating, but alcohol dehydrogenase converts it to formic acid, which inhibits mitochondrial cytochrome oxidase and produces a severe high-anion-gap metabolic acidosis with characteristic optic-nerve injury. As little as ~10 mL can cause blindness and ~30 mL can be fatal. Outbreaks recur when methanol adulterates informally produced alcoholic drinks. Reported mortality ranges roughly 8-36%, and 20-40% of survivors have permanent visual loss. Treatment blocks alcohol dehydrogenase with fomepizole (preferred) or ethanol; the landmark Brent fomepizole trial demonstrated safe, effective inhibition of methanol metabolism, and hemodialysis clears methanol and formate.",
    callout: "Educational, not medical advice. Non-sensational public-health note: the danger is delayed — a deceptively lucid interval precedes acidosis and blindness by hours, and antidote plus dialysis must start before vision is lost. Fomepizole is unavailable in many regions where outbreaks occur.",
