    id: "cost-caustic-ingestion", section: "3307", title: "Caustic (Corrosive) Ingestion", subtitle: "Degrades: esophageal/gastric integrity, swallowing, nutrition, survival",
    evidenceTag: "Strong",
    harm: { severity: 4, onset: "immediate", reversibility: "lasting" },
    degrades: ["esophageal and gastric integrity", "swallowing", "nutrition", "survival", "Bodily"],
    description: "Swallowing strong alkalis or acids injures the upper GI tract: alkalis (e.g., lye, drain cleaner) cause deep liquefactive necrosis that penetrates tissue, while acids cause coagulation necrosis. Endoscopic grade predicts outcome — higher-grade (2B, 3A) burns frequently progress to esophageal strictures. A systematic review/meta-analysis of corrosive esophageal injury reported pooled stricture formation around 24.7% and mortality around 6.2%, with grade III injury and intentional ingestion driving the worst outcomes. Long-term sequelae include chronic strictures, gastric outlet obstruction, and markedly elevated esophageal cancer risk decades later.",
