    id: "cost-cyanide-poisoning", section: "3308", title: "Cyanide Poisoning", subtitle: "Degrades: cellular respiration, CNS/cardiac oxygen use, survival",
    evidenceTag: "Strong",
    harm: { severity: 5, onset: "immediate", reversibility: "partial" },
    degrades: ["cellular respiration", "CNS oxygen utilization", "cardiac function", "survival", "Bodily"],
    description: "Cyanide binds cytochrome c oxidase (complex IV, cytochrome a3) in mitochondria, halting the electron transport chain so cells cannot use oxygen despite adequate delivery — forcing anaerobic metabolism, lactic acidosis, and rapid CNS/cardiac failure. High-dose exposure can kill within minutes. The most common real-world source is smoke inhalation from residential/industrial fires (combustion of nitrogen-containing plastics/wool); an elevated plasma lactate correlates with cyanide level and is a useful clinical marker. Hydroxocobalamin (Cyanokit) is the preferred antidote — it binds cyanide to form renally excreted cyanocobalamin without impairing oxygen-carrying capacity, unlike older methemoglobin-inducing kits.",
    callout: "Educational, not medical advice. Non-sensational note: in smoke-inhalation victims, unexplained lactic acidosis plus soot/altered mentation should prompt empiric hydroxocobalamin — waiting for a confirmatory cyanide level is not feasible given minutes-to-death kinetics.",
