    id: "cost-ethylene-glycol-poisoning", section: "3309", title: "Ethylene Glycol (Antifreeze) Poisoning", subtitle: "Degrades: kidney function, acid-base homeostasis, CNS integrity, survival",
    evidenceTag: "Strong",
    harm: { severity: 5, onset: "immediate", reversibility: "partial" },
    degrades: ["kidney function", "acid-base homeostasis", "CNS integrity", "survival", "Bodily"],
    description: "Ethylene glycol is metabolized by alcohol dehydrogenase to glycolaldehyde, glycolic acid, and finally oxalic acid, which combines with calcium to form insoluble calcium oxalate crystals — producing a high-anion-gap metabolic acidosis, hypocalcemia, and acute kidney injury. Toxicity classically evolves in stages: early CNS/inebriation (0.5-12 h), cardiopulmonary (12-24 h), then renal failure (24-72 h). The Brent fomepizole trial showed that blocking alcohol dehydrogenase halts formation of toxic metabolites; when fomepizole is started before severe acidemia or AKI, survival approaches 100%. Hemodialysis is added for established acidosis or renal injury.",
