    description: "Acute muscarinic-blockade poisoning — from diphenhydramine and other antihistamines, atropine/scopolamine, TCAs, and jimsonweed — produces the classic toxidrome: 'mad as a hatter (delirium), blind as a bat (mydriasis), red as a beet (flushing), hot as a hare (anhidrotic hyperthermia), dry as a bone (dry mucosa),' with tachycardia, urinary retention, and ileus. Severe diphenhydramine overdose (fatal cases reported >~20 mg/kg) adds seizures, coma, and — because diphenhydramine also blocks sodium channels at high dose — QRS widening. Physostigmine, an acetylcholinesterase inhibitor, can rapidly reverse pure anticholinergic delirium but is avoided when there is sodium-channel-blocker cardiotoxicity (risk of bradycardia/asystole/seizures).",
