    description: "Loss-chasing — continuing (or escalating) betting specifically to recover money already lost — is a distinct, well-replicated construct and a core marker of the slide from recreational gambling into disorder and financial wipeout. Breen & Zuckerman found self-reported impulsivity cleanly separated 'chasers' (who played until they lost their entire stake) from 'non-chasers' (who quit while ahead). Chasing is amplified by gambling-related cognitive distortions and near-miss/illusion-of-control beliefs, and predicts larger and faster losses than the base rate of the game alone.",
