    harm: { severity: 3, onset: "months", reversibility: "partial" },
    degrades: ["Meta-Cognitive", "Interpersonal", "Existential", "Financial"],
    description: "Kleine-Levin syndrome (KLS) is a rare relapsing-remitting disorder of episodic hypersomnia (patients sleep most of the day) with cognitive impairment, derealization, apathy and sometimes hyperphagia or hypersexuality. In Arnulf's systematic series of 108 patients, onset was typically in adolescence (mean ~15.7 years, 78% male), with a median ~19 episodes of ~13 days each, leaving patients incapacitated roughly 8 months over 14 years. Adolescent-onset disease has a measurable median duration around 12.9 years, but more than half of childhood- or adult-onset cases remain affected beyond 25 years.",
    callout: "Educational, not medical advice. KLS is very rare and often misdiagnosed as psychiatric illness or malingering; episode counts and durations come from referral series prone to selection bias, and no treatment has robust randomized evidence.",
