    id: "cost-sleep-related-eating-disorder", section: "3384", title: "Sleep-Related Eating Disorder (SRED) — Eating Asleep and the Zolpidem Trigger", subtitle: "Degrades: Bodily metabolic health, Interoceptive control, Emotional wellbeing, Financial/food-safety",
    evidenceTag: "Moderate",
    harm: { severity: 2, onset: "months", reversibility: "recovers" },
    degrades: ["Bodily", "Interoceptive", "Emotional", "Volitional"],
    description: "Sleep-related eating disorder (SRED) is an NREM parasomnia of recurrent, partly or fully amnestic eating during arousals from sleep, often of high-calorie or inedible items, leading to weight gain, metabolic risk, injury and distress. It is distinct from the fully awake night-eating syndrome. Prevalence ranges from ~4% (1-year ~2.4%) in surveys to 16.7% among hospitalized eating-disorder patients. It is strongly associated with sedative-hypnotics: in a review of 40 zolpidem-related cases, 65% were women using high doses (10-30 mg in 95%), and episodes typically ceased promptly after stopping the drug.",
