    harm: { severity: 1, onset: "immediate", reversibility: "recovers" },
    degrades: ["Emotional", "Interoceptive", "Intrapersonal"],
    description: "Recurrent isolated sleep paralysis is a REM-dissociation phenomenon in which a person wakes conscious but transiently unable to move or speak, often with intense fear and vivid (frequently threatening) hallucinations. It is common: a systematic review across ~36,533 people found lifetime episodes in ~7.6% of the general population, ~28.3% of students and ~31.9% of psychiatric patients; a larger 2024 meta-analysis of ~167,000 participants estimated overall prevalence around 30%. Rates are higher in students, shift workers and those with PTSD, anxiety or disrupted sleep schedules.",
