    description: "Aarts, Gollwitzer & Hassin's goal-contagion research shows people automatically adopt and pursue goals merely implied by observing others' behavior, and act on them persistently in new contexts — a goal-directed effect, not mimicry. Watching peers chase wealth, status, or acquisitions can silently install those goals as one's own. Notably, contagion does not occur when the observed pursuit looks unacceptable, so it is envy/comparison-selective: attractive-seeming rival goals get copied whether or not they fit the copier.",
