    degrades: ["hemostatic stability", "procedure safety", "hemodynamic stability", "survival"],
    description: "Cirrhosis produces a fragile 'rebalanced' hemostasis: parallel losses of pro- and anticoagulant factors leave patients simultaneously prone to bleeding and to thrombosis (portal vein thrombosis is the commonest thrombotic event). Crucially, conventional tests mislead — INR, platelet count, and fibrinogen do not reliably predict bleeding, and correcting a raised INR with fresh frozen plasma can raise portal pressure and worsen pressure-driven bleeding without improving hemostasis.",
    callout: "Educational, not medical advice. The dominant harm here is iatrogenic — treating the number (INR) rather than the patient. Prophylactic plasma/platelet transfusion before procedures is discouraged by current hepatology guidance.",
