    id: "cirrhotic-cardiomyopathy", section: "3523", title: "Cirrhotic Cardiomyopathy", subtitle: "Degrades: cardiac reserve, stress tolerance, perioperative survival",
    evidenceTag: "Moderate",
    harm: { severity: 3, onset: "years", reversibility: "partial" },
    degrades: ["cardiac reserve", "stress tolerance", "perioperative survival", "hemodynamic stability"],
    description: "Cirrhotic cardiomyopathy is latent cardiac dysfunction — impaired contractile response to stress, diastolic dysfunction, and electrophysiologic abnormalities (notably QT prolongation) — in the absence of other heart disease. Some feature is present in up to ~50-60% of patients with moderate-to-advanced cirrhosis. It is often silent at rest but decompensates under the stress of infection, TIPS, or transplant: 1-year survival falls with worsening diastolic dysfunction (95% with none, 79% grade I, 39% grade II), and post-transplant heart failure causes 7-21% mortality in the following months.",
