    description: "People anchor a compound probability near the probability of its individual components, so they overestimate the chance that a whole chain of steps all succeed (and underestimate that at least one of many risks fires). Bar-Hillel (1973) showed subjects systematically preferred conjunctive gambles whose true odds were lower, and Tversky & Kahneman (1974) tied it to anchoring: you start at each step's high success rate and adjust too little. A plan with seven near-independent 90%-likely steps has only ~0.9^7 ≈ 48% odds of finishing clean, yet planners intuit something close to 90% — which is why multi-milestone projects slip far more often than any single milestone would predict.",
