    degrades: ["Mathematical", "Logical", "Pattern-Recognition", "Strategic"],
    description: "When people predict an outcome from an imperfect indicator, they match the extremeness of the prediction to the extremeness of the evidence instead of regressing toward the base rate. Kahneman & Tversky (1973) showed that intuitive predictions ignore the unreliability of the input, so a dazzling pilot, an early sprint's velocity, or one star quarter gets extrapolated as if it were fully diagnostic of the future. In project forecasting this means non-regressive projections: the best early result is treated as the trend, and unusually good (or bad) starts are expected to continue rather than to drift back toward typical performance.",
    callout: "Distinct from inventing causal stories for noise (regression neglect in explanation); this is the forecasting side — over-extreme predictions from unreliable cues. The fix (regress toward the reference class) itself fails if you pick the wrong base rate.",
