    description: "Hall's high-context cultures encode meaning in shared context, indirection, and nonverbal cues, while low-context cultures load meaning into explicit words. When they meet, the low-context speaker reads indirect 'no' as agreement and the high-context speaker reads directness as rudeness — both miss the actual message. The construct is intuitive and widely taught, but its empirical foundation is weak: Kittler et al.'s systematic review found country HC/LC classifications rest on 'less-than-adequate' evidence with contradictory placements across studies.",
