    description: "Turn-taking rests on a near-universal target — minimize gap, avoid overlap (Stivers et al.'s 10-language study) — but the tolerated gap length and back-channel rate vary by culture within a ~250ms band and much more in listener signaling. Japanese speakers produce back-channels ('aizuchi') far more frequently than English or Mandarin speakers; a low-back-channel partner reads the frequent 'mm/hai' as interruption or false agreement, while the high-back-channel partner reads sparse feedback as disengagement. Small mismatches in when it's 'your turn' create over-talking or dead air across the whole conversation.",
