    description: "Ekman & Friesen's display rules govern how universal facial expressions are amplified, masked, or neutralized by social context, and these rules differ across cultures. Matsumoto's American–Japanese studies found Japanese participants rated masking of negative emotion toward in-group members more appropriate, and cultures differ in whether the eyes or mouth carry the decoding weight. Cross-culturally, a masked or muted expression is read as flat or evasive by an expressive-culture partner, while overt display reads as unprofessional or aggressive to a suppression-norm partner — mislabeling the other's actual feeling.",
