    id: "optimism-exceptional-longevity-85", section: "3669", title: "Optimism → Exceptional Longevity (Living Past 85)", subtitle: "practice: 'Bolsters clusters: Emotional, Intrapersonal, Existential'",
    evidenceTag: "Moderate",
    impact: { magnitude: 3, latency: "months", durability: "lasting", effort: "moderate" },
    feeds: ["Emotional", "Intrapersonal", "Existential"],
    description: "Across the Nurses' Health Study (69,744 women, 10-yr follow-up) and the VA Normative Aging Study (1,429 men, 30-yr), the most dispositionally optimistic people had an 11-15% longer lifespan and were 50-70% more likely to reach age 85 ('exceptional longevity') than the least optimistic. Associations persisted after adjusting for SES, health conditions, depression, social integration, and health behaviors — so it is not fully explained by optimists simply behaving healthier. This is a distinct endpoint (reaching very old age), not the general optimism-mortality link.",
