    description: "Pooled individual data on 34,485 adults 65+ (Studenski 2011, JAMA) showed usual walking speed predicts survival about as accurately as age, sex, mobility-aid use and self-reported function combined — the association is roughly linear, and each 0.1 m/s faster is worth measurably more expected years. A speed under ~0.8 m/s flags elevated risk; under ~0.6 m/s marks high vulnerability. Timing a comfortable 4-metre walk is a 10-second geriatric screen requiring no equipment.",
