    description: "A systematic review and meta-analysis of 22 studies (Robinson 2014, AJCN) found a slower eating rate lowers energy intake within a meal versus fast eating (SMD ~0.45; 95% CI 0.25-0.65) and raises satiety at meal end. Extending meal duration and chewing more gives satiety signals time to register before overshoot, and slow-eating habits are also cross-sectionally associated with lower body weight. A practical, no-cost lever on portion size and interoceptive fullness awareness.",
