    impact: { magnitude: 2, latency: "weeks", durability: "sustained", effort: "low" },
    feeds: ["Bodily", "mobility", "fall-risk awareness", "gait & balance"],
    description: "The TUG — rise from a chair, walk 3 m, turn, return, sit, timed — is a popular geriatric mobility screen, with ≥13.5 s often cited as 'fall risk'. But a systematic review/meta-analysis (Barry 2014, BMC Geriatrics; 25 studies) found it is poor at predicting falls in community-dwelling elders: pooled sensitivity only ~0.31, specificity ~0.74, and it was not a significant independent predictor (OR ~1.01). It captures mobility usefully but should not be used alone to rule falls in or out.",
    callout: "Educational, not medical advice. This is a deliberately honest 'anti-gem': the test is widely promoted for fall prediction on weaker evidence than its reputation implies. It measures mobility, which matters — but low sensitivity means a 'passing' time does not clear you of fall risk.",
