    feeds: ["Emotional", "Intrapersonal", "Interoceptive", "emotion regulation"],
    description: "Emotional granularity — the ability to parse feelings into precise, differentiated categories ('anxious and resentful and let down' vs. a global 'bad') — acts as a stress buffer. Kashdan, Barrett & McKnight (2015) synthesize evidence that high negative-emotion differentiators use fewer maladaptive strategies (binge drinking, aggression, self-harm) and show less neural reactivity to rejection. Nook et al. (2021, Clinical Psychological Science) found high differentiation buffered internalizing symptoms after stressful life events in adolescents.",
