    description: "Personal mastery — the generalized belief that one controls the forces shaping one's life (Pearlin & Schooler, 1978) — is a classic stress-buffer: it weakens the path from stressors to psychological distress by fostering active, problem-solving responses. Higher mastery is associated with better cardiometabolic profiles and, in several cohorts, reduced mortality risk, positioning it as a distinct resource from task-specific self-efficacy.",
