    feeds: ["Intrapersonal", "Emotional", "Meta-Cognitive", "psychological distancing"],
    description: "Reflecting on a stressor from a distanced, fly-on-the-wall vantage — or using your own name and 'you' instead of 'I' ('Why is Sam upset?') — dampens emotional reactivity and rumination. Kross et al. (2014, JPSP) showed non-first-person self-talk improved stress appraisal and performance under social threat. Moser et al. (2017, Scientific Reports) found third-person self-talk reduced an ERP marker of emotional reactivity within a second, without recruiting effortful cognitive control — i.e., a nearly free regulation lever.",
