    description: "Where money is genuinely scarce, giving cash — no strings — reliably improves subjective wellbeing and mental health. A meta-analysis by McGuire, Kaiser & Bach-Mortensen (2022) across low- and middle-income-country studies found cash transfers produced a real, dose-dependent improvement in mental health and subjective wellbeing (pooled effect roughly a tenth of a standard deviation; larger transfers, larger effects). The GiveDirectly RCT (Haushofer & Shapiro, 2016) showed unconditional transfers in Kenya raised happiness and life satisfaction and lowered stress-hormone (cortisol) levels. This is the cleanest causal evidence that reducing scarcity — not just correlating with wealth — lifts wellbeing.",
