    description: "A few drops of heel-prick blood in the first days of life screen for congenital hypothyroidism (~1 in 2,500–3,000), phenylketonuria (~1 in 10,000–15,000) and dozens of other treatable conditions — disorders that, if caught late, cause permanent intellectual disability but if caught in the first weeks are largely preventable with thyroxine or a modified diet. Cost-effectiveness analyses find screening is dominant (more QALYs at lower cost), with congenital-hypothyroidism screening returning roughly \\$2.5–\\$7.8 saved per dollar spent. One of the highest-ROI preventive programs in all of medicine.",
