    id: "maternal-tdap-pertussis-pregnancy", section: "3823", title: "Maternal Tdap in Pregnancy (Whooping-Cough Shield)", subtitle: "practice: 'Bolsters clusters: newborn pertussis immunity, maternal antibody transfer, community protection'",
    evidenceTag: "Strong",
    impact: { magnitude: 4, latency: "weeks", durability: "sustained", effort: "low" },
    feeds: ["Bodily", "Parental", "Community"],
    description: "Giving Tdap in the third trimester passes anti-pertussis antibodies across the placenta, protecting the newborn during the deadly window before the infant's own vaccines start at 2 months. A large Kaiser Permanente study (~148,000 newborns) found ~91% vaccine effectiveness against pertussis in the first 2 months and ~69% across the first year, with England's program showing ~90% protection against hospitalized infant pertussis and high efficacy against infant pertussis death. Among the clearest examples of vaccinating one person to protect another.",
