    id: "maternal-rsv-immunization-nirsevimab", section: "3824", title: "RSV Protection for Infants (Maternal Vaccine or Nirsevimab)", subtitle: "practice: 'Bolsters clusters: newborn RSV immunity, prevented hospitalization, respiratory resilience'",
    evidenceTag: "Strong",
    impact: { magnitude: 4, latency: "weeks", durability: "sustained", effort: "low" },
    feeds: ["Bodily", "Community"],
    description: "RSV is the leading cause of infant hospitalization; two new tools blunt it. The maternal RSVpreF vaccine in late pregnancy cut severe RSV lower-respiratory illness by ~69–82% in early infancy (MATISSE trial), and the long-acting monoclonal antibody nirsevimab given to the infant showed ~74–83% efficacy in trials and ~90% effectiveness against RSV hospitalization in real-world CDC data. Either strategy protects the baby through its first RSV season.",
    callout: "Maternal vaccine and infant nirsevimab are alternative routes to the same protection (generally one or the other, not both); head-to-head data suggest nirsevimab yields somewhat greater reduction in severe outcomes. Educational, not medical advice.",
