    id: "prenatal-alcohol-avoidance-fasd", section: "3825", title: "Avoiding Alcohol in Pregnancy (FASD Prevention)", subtitle: "practice: 'Bolsters clusters: fetal neurodevelopment, cognitive integrity, behavioral regulation'",
    evidenceTag: "Strong",
    impact: { magnitude: 4, latency: "months", durability: "lasting", effort: "moderate" },
    feeds: ["Logical", "Bodily", "Parental"],
    description: "Prenatal alcohol is a direct teratogen and the leading preventable cause of neurodevelopmental disability: fetal alcohol spectrum disorders affect an estimated 1–5% of US children — more common than autism — causing lifelong cognitive, learning and behavioral impairment. There is no established safe amount or safe trimester, so complete abstinence prevents 100% of the harm. Brief, motivational-interviewing-style interventions in prenatal care measurably reduce drinking in pregnancy.",
    callout: "The causal harm of alcohol is not in doubt, but the effectiveness of counseling interventions to change behavior is moderate; framing should reduce shame while conveying that abstinence is the only fully protective option. Educational, not medical advice.",
