    id: "iodine-sufficiency-pregnancy", section: "3827", title: "Iodine Sufficiency in Pregnancy", subtitle: "practice: 'Bolsters clusters: fetal brain development, verbal IQ, cognitive achievement'",
    evidenceTag: "Moderate",
    impact: { magnitude: 4, latency: "months", durability: "lasting", effort: "low" },
    feeds: ["Logical", "Linguistic", "Bodily"],
    description: "Iodine is the raw material for thyroid hormone that builds the fetal brain; deficiency in early pregnancy is the world's leading preventable cause of intellectual disability. Severe deficiency causes cretinism, and even mild-to-moderate maternal deficiency is linked to lower offspring verbal IQ and reading scores (Bath et al., ALSPAC). Meta-analytic estimates put the IQ penalty of deficiency at ~12–13.5 points, and adequate intake — via iodized salt or a prenatal supplement — prevents it cheaply.",
