    id: "universal-newborn-hearing-screening", section: "3828", title: "Universal Newborn Hearing Screening (Early Detection)", subtitle: "practice: 'Bolsters clusters: spoken language, reading comprehension, parent-infant communication'",
    evidenceTag: "Moderate",
    impact: { magnitude: 4, latency: "months", durability: "lasting", effort: "low" },
    feeds: ["Linguistic", "Parental", "Interpersonal"],
    description: "Screening every newborn's hearing (OAE/ABR) before discharge, so permanent hearing loss is confirmed and intervention started by ~6 months instead of at age 2–3, exploits the critical window for spoken-language wiring. Kennedy et al. (NEJM 2006) found children whose hearing loss was confirmed by 9 months had significantly higher language scores, and Pimperton et al. found the reading-comprehension advantage persisted and grew into the teenage years. Roughly 1–3 per 1,000 newborns have permanent bilateral loss, and outcomes hinge on how early it is caught.",
