    description: "Actively experiencing something that violates your expectations of how the world works — not merely novel, but rule-breaking — boosts cognitive flexibility, the engine of creative ideation. Ritter et al. (2012) had participants live through schema-violating events (e.g., a VR world where gravity behaved wrongly, or making a sandwich in reversed order) and found greater post-task cognitive flexibility than novelty-matched or passive-observation controls. Crucially, ACTIVE involvement in the anomaly — not just watching it — drives the effect.",
