    id: "inspiratory-muscle-strength-training-bp", section: "3930", title: "Inspiratory Muscle Strength Training (IMST)", subtitle: "practice: 'Bolsters clusters: Blood-Pressure Control, Cardiovascular longevity, Respiratory muscle strength'",
    evidenceTag: "Emerging",
    impact: { magnitude: 3, latency: "weeks", durability: "sustained", effort: "low" },
    feeds: ["Blood-Pressure Control", "Cardiovascular longevity", "Respiratory muscle strength", "Interoceptive"],
    description: "High-resistance, low-volume breathing training — roughly 30 resisted breaths a day at 75% of maximal inspiratory pressure — lowers blood pressure with minimal time cost. Craighead's trials in midlife/older adults with above-normal BP found systolic pressure fell ~9 mmHg and diastolic ~4 mmHg after 6 weeks, alongside improved endothelial function and nitric-oxide bioavailability, with benefits partly persisting after training stopped. The effect size rivals aerobic exercise for a ~5-minute daily practice.",
