    degrades: ["Survival / longevity", "Cardiovascular reserve", "Exercise capacity", "Independence"],
    description: "Reduced forced expiratory volume in one second (FEV1) is one of the strongest, most generalizable predictors of death — often outperforming conventional risk factors. In the PURE analysis across 21 countries, impaired FEV1 was a strong independent predictor of all-cause mortality and cardiovascular events, with the authors estimating it relates to roughly 1 in 4 deaths and 1 in 6 cardiovascular events. The Buffalo Health Study showed FEV1 predicting mortality across 29 years even in never-smokers without symptoms.",
