    description: "Frequent trading feels like control but shreds returns through costs. In Barber & Odean's study of 66,465 brokerage households (1991-1996), the most active traders earned ~11.4% net annually vs ~18.5% for the least active and 17.9% for the market — a ~7-point gap driven almost entirely by transaction costs, not stock-picking. Overconfidence best explains why people trade so much, and a related study found men trade more than women and underperform them.",
    callout: "Educational, not financial advice. Robust and replicated, but from a 1990s high-commission era; zero-commission trading lowers direct costs (while arguably encouraging even more churn). The core lesson — turnover is a tax on returns — stands. Buy-and-hold indexing is the direct antidote.",
