    weakness: { threat: 3, weakLines: ["Emotional", "Meta-Cognitive"], degree: "moderate contributor", onset: "months", reversibility: "recovers" },
    description: "Kirsch et al. (2008, PLoS Medicine) meta-analyzed all FDA trial data for four newer antidepressants (fluoxetine, venlafaxine, nefazodone, paroxetine). Drugs beat placebo statistically, but the drug–placebo gap was clinically trivial at mild-to-moderate severity and only crossed clinical-significance thresholds at the very severe end — most of the observed improvement was matched in the placebo arm.",
    callout: "Contested (later network meta-analyses like Cipriani 2018 argue antidepressants do beat placebo overall), but the honest core stands: for milder depression, expectancy/placebo accounts for most of the response, so 'the pill worked' overstates drug-specific effect. Educational, not medical advice.",
