    id: "anticoagulation-atrial-fibrillation-stroke", section: "4211", title: "Anticoagulation in Atrial Fibrillation — Stroke Prevention", subtitle: "practice: 'Bolsters clusters: Stroke Prevention, Cardiovascular Event Prevention, Strategic-about-health'",
    evidenceTag: "Strong",
    impact: { magnitude: 5, latency: "days", durability: "sustained", effort: "moderate" },
    feeds: ["Stroke prevention", "Bodily", "Strategic", "Systemic"],
    description: "In atrial fibrillation, oral anticoagulation is one of the highest-yield preventive drug classes. A meta-analysis of 29 trials (n=28,044) found adjusted-dose warfarin cut stroke by 64% (RR reduction, 95% CI 49–74%) vs control, and by ~39% vs antiplatelet therapy; antiplatelets alone gave only ~22%. Modern direct oral anticoagulants (DOACs) match warfarin's efficacy with less intracranial bleeding and no monitoring.",
    callout: "Educational, not medical advice. The trade-off is bleeding risk; net benefit depends on stroke risk (e.g., CHA2DS2-VASc) vs bleeding risk. Not for low-risk lone AF — this is a risk-stratified decision.",
