    description: "Batson's coin-flip studies expose a common self-deception: given a private coin to fairly assign a good vs. bad task, ~85–90% of those who 'flipped' still gave themselves the good task — the flip was theater. Yet coin-flippers rated their choice as MORE moral than non-flippers. The engine isn't misperceiving the act as fair; it's avoiding comparing one's behavior to the standard at all. A pervasive vulnerability to feeling virtuous while acting selfishly.",
