    description: "People rate themselves as more just, honest, and moral than the average person — and Tappin & McKay (2017) found this self-inflation is stronger and more irrational for moral traits than for competence or warmth. Uniquely, it's the domain where positive illusion is most extreme, and it wasn't tied to healthy self-esteem. This blind spot fuels self-righteousness, poor moral self-correction, and dismissal of others' ethics.",
