    description: "An honest wrinkle in numeracy: frequencies help you *compute* (natural frequencies aid Bayesian reasoning) but can distort how *threatening* a risk feels. Slovic, Monahan & MacGregor (2000) found clinicians judged a patient framed as '20 out of 100 will commit violence' as more dangerous than one framed as '20% likely' — the mathematically identical figure — because the frequency format conjures vivid images of specific victims and neglects the denominator. So the same reframing that improves accuracy in diagnosis can inflate fear in risk perception; the weakness is not noticing which effect is operating.",
