    description: "Carstensen's socioemotional selectivity theory holds that when time horizons feel limited — through age, illness, or an imposed ending — people reprioritize away from information-gathering toward emotionally meaningful goals and close relationships, pruning peripheral ties. This reprioritization is well-documented and helps explain older adults' often-better emotional wellbeing. Deliberately invoking a limited horizon (e.g. imagining a move, an ending) can reorient attention toward what matters.",
