    description: "The standard way of measuring post-traumatic growth asks survivors to retrospectively rate how much they have grown — and that number often does not match actual pre-to-post change. Frazier et al. (2009), comparing genuine longitudinal change to the Post-Traumatic Growth Inventory, found perceived growth was largely unrelated to actual growth, and perceived growth was associated with more distress while actual growth tracked less distress. Reported 'growth' is partly a coping narrative, not verified change.",
