    description: "Golder & Tellis's historical analysis of ~500 brands across 50 categories found the durable winners were usually not the pioneers but early leaders who entered on average ~13 years after the pioneer, once the market and technology had been de-risked. Fast-followers learn from the pioneer's mistakes, enter with a better product, and capture higher long-run share. Letting someone else pay to educate the market can be the stronger position.",
