    description: "Periodically resetting a portfolio to its target weights (e.g. selling winners to buy laggards) mainly HOLDS your risk level constant rather than boosting return. Willenbrock (2011) formalizes a 'diversification return' that arises among volatile, low-correlation, similar-return assets; Bernstein's 'rebalancing bonus' shows the same. But across genuinely different-return assets like stocks and bonds, rebalancing usually LOWERS long-run return (you trim the higher-returning asset) while reducing volatility and drift.",
    callout: "Educational, not financial advice. The 'bonus' is often overstated: it is real only under specific conditions (similar expected returns, high volatility, low correlation). Its dependable value is behavioral and risk-control — keeping you from drifting into an unintended, riskier allocation — not a reliable extra return.",
