    degrades: ["net worth", "cash flow", "Mathematical/compounding intuition", "financial resilience"],
    description: "Revolving credit-card balances compound interest AGAINST you — the same exponential math that builds wealth, reversed — and paying only the minimum stretches repayment over years while multiplying total interest. Keys & Wang (2019, JFE) found a large share of borrowers anchor near the contractual minimum, dramatically slowing paydown. Stewart (2009) showed experimentally that merely PRINTING a minimum-payment figure on a statement lowers the amount people choose to pay, an anchoring effect that raises lifetime interest cost.",
    callout: "Educational, not financial advice. This is the high-interest-debt mirror of compounding: at ~20%+ APR, minimum payments can keep a balance alive for a decade-plus. Paying more than the minimum — ideally the full balance — is close to a guaranteed, tax-free 'return' equal to the card's APR.",
    sources: [
      { cite: "Keys, B. J., & Wang, J. (2019). Minimum payments and debt paydown in consumer credit cards. Journal of Financial Economics, 131(3), 528-548.", note: "A meaningful minority of accounts pay at or near the minimum, sharply extending payoff time and total interest; anchoring, not just liquidity, drives it. [Strong — large administrative dataset]", link: "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jfineco.2018.09.009", kind: "doi" },
      { cite: "Stewart, N. (2009). The Cost of Anchoring on Credit-Card Minimum Repayments. Psychological Science, 20(1), 39-41.", note: "Displaying a minimum-payment figure causally lowered chosen repayment amounts in experiments, increasing interest paid. [Strong — controlled experiment]", link: "https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1467-9280.2008.02255.x", kind: "doi" },
    ],
