    id: "strategic-anger-expression-negotiation", section: "4360", title: "Expressing Anger to Get Concessions (Short-Term Gain, Relational Cost)", subtitle: "cost: 'Degrades: trust, long-term cooperation, relationship durability'",
    evidenceTag: "Moderate",
    harm: { severity: 2, onset: "immediate", reversibility: "partial" },
    degrades: ["Interpersonal", "Adversarial", "Strategic", "Leadership"],
    description: "Van Kleef, De Dreu & Manstead showed opponents concede more to an angry counterpart than a happy one — they read anger as a signal the other is near their limit and adjust demands downward. But this is a double-edged tool: the leverage evaporates when the angry party can't back it up, and later work finds displayed anger sows distrust, invites retaliation and hardball, and damages the ongoing relationship — especially when the anger reads as inauthentic or the target has power or an alternative.",
    callout: "Not an endorsement — this is a trust-corroding tactic dressed as a communication skill. Concession effects are moderated by power, authenticity and culture, and the relational and reputational costs typically outlast the one-time gain. Included honestly because it is widely used, not because it repairs conflict.",
