    degrades: ["Emotional", "Interpersonal", "Volitional", "Adversarial"],
    description: "The intuition that expressing anger 'discharges' it is largely false. In Bushman's classic experiments, angered participants who vented on a punching bag while thinking about the provocateur stayed angrier and were subsequently more aggressive (louder, longer noise blasts) than those who did nothing; simply sitting quietly for two minutes was more effective than venting. Rumination-while-venting rehearses the anger rather than releasing it. For conflict repair this reframes 'let me vent first' and aggressive catharsis (or angry group co-rumination) as counterproductive.",
    callout: "Educational, not therapeutic advice. This targets aggressive/ruminative venting; calmly naming a feeling or problem-solving disclosure is different. The finding is well-replicated, but distraction and time-outs — not suppression — are the supported alternatives.",
    sources: [
      { cite: "Bushman, B. J. (2002). Does venting anger feed or extinguish the flame? Catharsis, rumination, distraction, anger, and aggressive responding. Personality and Social Psychology Bulletin, 28(6), 724-731.", note: "Venting-with-rumination increased anger and later aggression vs distraction or doing nothing — directly contradicting catharsis theory. [Strong — controlled experiment, widely replicated conclusion]", link: "https://doi.org/10.1177/0146167202289002", kind: "doi" },
    ],
