    description: "Sezer, Gino & Norton's nine studies (including a diary study and a field experiment) show humblebragging — 'so exhausted from being asked to speak everywhere' — is common but ineffective: it lowers likability, perceived competence, and compliance with requests, because observers read it as insincere. Strikingly, it underperforms straightforward bragging and even plain complaining; the complaint-framed version ('ugh, why do people...') is the worst of all. Sincerity, not modesty-signaling, is what earns credit.",
