    id: "surprise-of-reaching-out", section: "4364", title: "Reaching Out (Check-Ins Land Better Than You Expect)", subtitle: "practice: 'Bolsters clusters: friendship maintenance, felt appreciation, social connection'",
    evidenceTag: "Moderate",
    impact: { magnitude: 2, latency: "days", durability: "transient", effort: "low" },
    feeds: ["Interpersonal", "Community", "Emotional", "Social-Perceptual"],
    description: "People chronically underestimate how much a small, unprompted check-in — a text, a note, a little gift — is appreciated by the recipient. Across experiments with ~5,900 participants, Liu et al. found initiators focus on the message content while responders are gripped by the pleasant surprise of being thought of, which drives higher-than-predicted appreciation. The gap is largest for more socially distant contacts and more surprising contexts — precisely the reach-outs we most hesitate to send.",
