    id: "feedback-sandwich-ineffective", section: "4366", title: "The 'Feedback Sandwich' (Praise-Criticism-Praise)", subtitle: "cost: 'Degrades: feedback clarity, behavior change, perceived sincerity'",
    evidenceTag: "Mixed",
    harm: { severity: 2, onset: "immediate", reversibility: "recovers" },
    degrades: ["Leadership", "Rhetorical", "Interpersonal", "Linguistic"],
    description: "Wrapping criticism between two compliments is one of the most-taught feedback formulas, yet the empirical case for it is weak. Studies find sandwiching alters perceptions — recipients often like the delivery — but does not reliably improve performance over direct feedback, and the framing praise is frequently discounted as insincere 'padding' that muddies which message matters. Recipients may latch onto the praise and dismiss the correction, defeating the purpose.",
    callout: "Educational, not a mandate. The evidence base is thin and mixed rather than a clean debunk: perception effects are real but performance effects are largely null/small across few studies. The honest read is that a rote sandwich adds little; specific, timely, candid feedback delivered with care is the better-supported alternative.",
