    id: "expectancy-value-utility", section: "4376", title: "Expectancy × Value — Making a Task Feel Useful", subtitle: "practice: 'Bolsters clusters: Volitional, Intrapersonal, Strategic'",
    evidenceTag: "Moderate",
    impact: { magnitude: 3, latency: "weeks", durability: "sustained", effort: "low" },
    feeds: ["Volitional", "Intrapersonal", "Strategic"],
    description: "Expectancy-value theory holds that motivation is the product of how much you expect to succeed and how much you value the task — if either is near zero, effort collapses. A short, cheap intervention leverages this: having students write about how course material is personally useful (a utility-value manipulation) raised interest and grades, especially for those with low success expectations. Hulleman & Harackiewicz (2009) found a ~0.8 grade-point lift for initially low-performing students in a randomized high-school science trial.",
    callout: "Utility-value effects are strongest for low-expectancy/low-performing people and can be negligible or slightly negative for confident high-performers. Some classroom replications are smaller; scale-up effects shrink.",
    sources: [
      { cite: "Hulleman, C. S., & Harackiewicz, J. M. (2009). Promoting interest and performance in high school science classes. Science, 326(5958), 1410-1412.", note: "Randomized; writing about personal relevance raised interest and grades for low-expectancy students (~0.8 GPA points). Benefit concentrated in low-confidence students. [Strong — RCT]", link: "https://doi.org/10.1126/science.1177067", kind: "doi" },
      { cite: "Eccles, J. S., & Wigfield, A. (2002). Motivational beliefs, values, and goals. Annual Review of Psychology, 53, 109-132.", note: "Reviews the expectancy-value framework of achievement motivation. Theory synthesis, not a single effect size. [Moderate — review]", link: "https://doi.org/10.1146/annurev.psych.53.100901.135153", kind: "doi" },
    ],
