    description: "Thorndyke & Hayes-Roth showed the source of spatial learning shapes the mental representation: maps yield 'survey knowledge' (bird's-eye relations, good for straight-line distance and relative location), while navigation yields 'route knowledge' (good for orienting to unseen landmarks and estimating travel distances). People who learn only by traveling struggle with survey-type judgments even after months, so deliberately studying maps builds a flexible, allocentric cognitive map.",
