    evidenceTag: "Mixed",
    harm: { severity: 2, onset: "immediate", reversibility: "recovers" },
    degrades: ["Interpersonal", "Social-Perceptual", "Emotional"],
    description: "Przybylski & Weinstein 2013 had 34 stranger dyads talk for 10 minutes with a phone merely visible on a nearby table (vs a notebook); the phone lowered rated closeness and partner trust, strongest for personally meaningful topics. Dwyer, Kushlev & Dunn 2018 ran a field experiment with 304 diners — phones on the table made meals with friends/family less enjoyable, mediated by greater distraction (a small effect), replicated in experience-sampling. This is distinct from the cognitive 'brain drain' and from one-off phone-snubbing.",
