    description: "When you rate 'how well have I learned this?' immediately after studying, you are badly miscalibrated — the cue and answer are both in front of you, creating a foresight bias and an illusion of competence (Koriat & Bjork 2005). The robust remedy: make the judgment resemble the eventual test. Delaying judgments of learning until after a short gap, and giving yourself actual retrieval practice, sharply improves calibration between felt and real learning (Koriat & Bjork 2006).",
    callout: "The fix is specific — delay the judgment and test yourself; simply 'trying harder' to introspect during study does not work because the illusion comes from the study context itself masking future retrieval difficulty.",
