    id: "feeling-of-knowing-tip-of-tongue", section: "4439", title: "Feeling-of-Knowing & Tip-of-the-Tongue as Metacognitive Signals", subtitle: "practice: 'Bolsters clusters: memory monitoring, retrieval strategy, Meta-Cognitive accuracy'",
    evidenceTag: "Strong",
    impact: { magnitude: 2, latency: "days", durability: "sustained", effort: "low" },
    feeds: ["Meta-Cognitive", "Linguistic", "Interoceptive"],
    description: "Feeling-of-knowing (FOK) — the sense that an unrecalled item is nonetheless known — is a genuinely diagnostic metacognitive signal: Hart (1965) showed people were far more likely to recognize items they said they 'knew' than items they didn't. Tip-of-the-tongue states (Brown & McNeill 1966) reliably carry partial information (first letter, syllable count, related words), evidence that retrieval monitoring tracks real, partly-accessible memory. Used well, these feelings tell you when to keep searching, when to look it up, and when to stop.",
    callout: "FOK is informative but imperfect — it is partly cue-driven inference, not direct memory access, so a strong feeling of knowing can still be wrong; treat it as a probabilistic tip, not a guarantee.",
