    feeds: ["Meta-Cognitive", "Logical"],
    description: "Alter, Oppenheimer, Epley & Eyre (2007) reported that presenting problems in a degraded, hard-to-read font boosted correct answers on the Cognitive Reflection Test by triggering slower, analytic (System 2) processing. The effect became a popular 'desirable difficulty' talking point — but it largely failed to replicate: Thompson et al. (2013) found no reliable disfluency benefit (a small effect only among the highest-ability reasoners), and Meyer et al. (2015) reported null effects across roughly 13 experiments and a large sample.",
