    description: "Blanchflower reports a well-being U-shape — a midlife dip bottoming around age 47–48 then rising — in 132–145 countries after controlling for income, education, and marital/employment status. But this is genuinely contested: Galambos, Krahn, Lachman and colleagues show the U-shape often flattens or vanishes once controls are added or when the same people are followed longitudinally, arguing the pattern is an artifact of covariate choice and cross-sectional (cohort) confounds.",
