    description: "The late-life wellbeing upswing does not last to the end. Longitudinal panel data (e.g., ~2,910 deceased German SOEP participants; replicated in UK/US) show a 'terminal decline': life satisfaction drops sharply in the final years, typically beginning 3–5 years before death, with the rate of decline roughly tripling at a transition point ~4 years pre-death and falling about a full standard deviation. It tracks distance-to-death more than chronological age.",
    callout: "Distinct from normal aging: this is proximity to death, not age itself, driving the collapse — 'something is seriously wrong at the end of life.' Onset timing varies widely; some people show little terminal decline. Socially engaged people decline less steeply.",
