    description: "The Health Belief Model (perceived susceptibility, severity, benefits, barriers) is one of the most widely taught behaviour-change theories, but its predictive validity is weak. Carpenter's (2010) meta-analysis (18 longitudinal studies, ~2,700 people) found perceived barriers (r = -.21) and benefits were the only usable predictors, while perceived susceptibility was essentially null (r = .05) and severity weak. Designing programs around 'making people feel at risk' has little empirical support.",
