    description: "Loewenstein's information-gap theory frames curiosity as an aversive feeling triggered when attention lands on a gap between what you know and what you want to know — and it peaks when you already have a little knowledge (not zero, not saturation). fMRI work (Kang et al. 2009) shows that greater curiosity about a trivia answer predicts more caudate/reward activation and better later recall of the answer, so deliberately opening a small gap before learning primes engagement. Practically: pose the question before giving the answer.",
