    description: "Every adaptive agent trades off exploiting a known reward against exploring options that might be better. Wilson, Cohen and colleagues show humans solve this with two distinct strategies — directed exploration (deliberately sampling the more uncertain/informative option) and random exploration (injecting decision noise) — and, crucially, they explore MORE when the decision horizon is longer, i.e., when there's time to cash in what they learn. The takeaway: match your exploration to how much future you have to benefit from it.",
