    id: "truth-default-bias", section: "4539", title: "Truth-Default Bias (We Assume People Are Honest)", subtitle: "weakness: 'Degrades: Adversarial, Social-Perceptual, Meta-Cognitive, Street-Smarts'",
    evidenceTag: "Moderate",
    weakness: { threat: 5, weakLines: ["Adversarial", "Social-Perceptual", "Street-Smarts"], degree: "moderate contributor", onset: "immediate", reversibility: "recovers" },
    degrades: ["Adversarial", "Social-Perceptual", "Meta-Cognitive", "Street-Smarts"],
    description: "Levine's Truth-Default Theory holds that humans default to believing what they are told and only leave that default when a trigger makes deception salient. This truth-bias is efficient and usually adaptive — in normal life most messages ARE honest — but it is exactly what liars, scammers, and misinformation exploit: absent a specific reason for suspicion, we accept claims at face value. It also explains why humans are only slightly better than chance at detecting deception.",
    callout: "Levine's key nuance is that truth-bias is not simply a flaw — it yields high accuracy in a mostly-honest world and abandoning it wholesale (chronic suspicion) has real costs. The vulnerability is situational: high-deception environments (feeds, cold-calls, ads).",
    sources: [
      { cite: "Levine, T. R. (2014). Truth-Default Theory (TDT): A theory of human deception and deception detection. Journal of Language and Social Psychology, 33(4), 378-392.", note: "Formalises the truth-default/truth-bias and its adaptive value; explains near-chance deception detection. Limitation: theoretical synthesis; truth-bias magnitude varies by context. [Moderate — theory grounded in deception-detection literature]", link: "https://doi.org/10.1177/0261927X14535916", kind: "doi" },
    ],
