    id: "trained-facilitator-brainstorming", section: "4558", title: "A Trained Facilitator Rescues Group Brainstorming", subtitle: "practice: 'Bolsters clusters: Creative, Leadership, Interpersonal, Entrepreneurial'",
    evidenceTag: "Moderate",
    impact: { magnitude: 3, latency: "days", durability: "transient", effort: "moderate" },
    feeds: ["Creative", "Leadership", "Interpersonal", "Entrepreneurial"],
    description: "The usual verbal-brainstorming deficit versus solo-then-pool can be partly closed by adding a skilled facilitator. Oxley, Dzindolet & Paulus (1996) found interacting groups led by a highly trained facilitator generated as many ideas as nominal groups and more than unfacilitated groups. The facilitator's job is mechanical: cut tangents, restart the group when it stalls, and pull ideas from quiet members — countering social loafing and premature stopping.",
