    id: "optimal-analogical-distance", section: "4562", title: "How Far Is Too Far? Analogical Distance", subtitle: "practice: 'Bolsters clusters: Creative, Pattern-Recognition, Entrepreneurial'",
    evidenceTag: "Mixed",
    impact: { magnitude: 2, latency: "days", durability: "transient", effort: "moderate" },
    feeds: ["Creative", "Pattern-Recognition", "Entrepreneurial"],
    description: "Cross-domain analogies fuel novelty, but 'farther is always better' is not settled. Some design and open-innovation studies find distant/biological analogies raise novelty; others report an inverted-U where moderately distant sources beat both near and very-far ones — because extremely distant analogies become hard to map onto the problem. The honest read: analogical distance helps novelty on average, but the relationship is domain-dependent and sometimes non-monotonic.",
    callout: "Evidence is genuinely mixed and moderated by task, expertise, and whether a mapping hint is provided; don't assume maximal distance maximizes usable ideas — very far analogies can raise novelty while lowering feasibility.",
    sources: [
      { cite: "Chan, J., Fu, K., Schunn, C., Cagan, J., Wood, K., & Kotovsky, K. (2011). On the benefits and pitfalls of analogies for innovative design. Journal of Mechanical Design, 133(8), 081004.", note: "Far-field analogies aided novelty but with pitfalls; effect of distance depends on timing and how the analogy is presented. [Moderate — experiments]", link: "https://doi.org/10.1115/1.4004396", kind: "doi" },
      { cite: "Fu, K., Chan, J., Cagan, J., Kotovsky, K., Schunn, C., & Wood, K. (2013). The meaning of 'near' and 'far': The impact of structuring design databases and the effect of distance of analogy on design output. Journal of Mechanical Design, 135(2), 021007.", note: "Found a non-monotonic distance effect — near analogies could outperform very far ones — challenging 'farther is always better.' [Moderate]", link: "https://doi.org/10.1115/1.4023158", kind: "doi" },
    ],
