    description: "Matz, Gladstone & Stillwell analyzed 76,000+ real bank transactions and found people are happier when their spending matches their personality (e.g. extraverts spending on social outings, conscientious people on health). The fit between spending category and Big-Five profile predicted life satisfaction more strongly than either total income or total spending, and a follow-up field experiment giving people 'fit' vouchers nudged happiness upward. The lesson: how well money is spent matters more than how much.",
