    impact: { magnitude: 3, latency: "days", durability: "sustained", effort: "low" },
    feeds: ["Interpersonal", "Emotional", "Community", "Financial", "Existential"],
    description: "Caprariello & Reis show the experiences-beat-possessions effect is largely a connection effect. Across four studies, spending on socially shared experiences was valued most, but solitary experiences were rated no happier than material goods — the advantage of experiences comes substantially from doing them with other people. Practical read: prioritize discretionary money on things you do with others, and the experiential premium reliably shows up.",
