    impact: { magnitude: 3, latency: "weeks", durability: "sustained", effort: "moderate" },
    feeds: ["Linguistic", "Meta-Cognitive", "Logical"],
    description: "Distinct from writing for achievement generally, Graham & Hebert's (2011) meta-analysis found that having students write about a text they read — summarizing it, answering/generating written questions, taking and rewording notes, or analyzing it — reliably improved comprehension of that text, with effect sizes commonly in the 0.30–0.50 range across activity types. Simply increasing how much students write, and teaching writing skills, also improved reading comprehension and fluency.",
