    feeds: ["Strategic", "Adversarial", "Interpersonal", "Moral"],
    description: "In Axelrod's two computer tournaments of the iterated prisoner's dilemma, the simplest strategy — Tit-for-Tat (cooperate first, then copy the opponent's last move) — won both times against strategies designed to beat it. Axelrod distilled why: successful strategies are nice (never defect first), retaliatory (punish defection immediately), forgiving (return to cooperation once the other side does), and clear (easy to read). It is a genuinely useful heuristic for repeated interaction, not a universal optimum — later work showed it can be beaten in specific ecologies and is fragile to noise.",
