    description: "In strictly competitive (zero-sum) games where any predictable pattern can be exploited, the optimal play is to randomize — von Neumann's minimax theorem says mix your actions so the opponent can't gain from reading you, equalizing your payoff across choices. This is one of the few game-theory predictions confirmed in real high-stakes behavior: Palacios-Huerta analyzed 1,417 professional soccer penalty kicks and found kickers and keepers mix almost exactly as minimax predicts — success rates equalized across sides (~80% goals) and choices were serially independent (no exploitable patterns).",
