    feeds: ["Strategic", "Rhetorical", "Interpersonal", "Adversarial"],
    description: "Crawford & Sobel modeled 'cheap talk' — costless, non-binding messages from a better-informed sender to a receiver. Their key result: how much true information gets transmitted depends entirely on how aligned the two parties' interests are. When incentives are close, communication is fine-grained and credible; as interests diverge, the sender can only convey coarse, partitioned information, and with severe conflict no credible communication is possible at all. It explains why advice, forecasts, and negotiations carry information only to the extent that interests overlap.",
